# gorb-os — agent notes

Independent ESP-IDF firmware project (see `README.md`). This file only records
the gotchas that cost real time on the bench; it does not restate the README.

## Commit messages carry no attribution trailer

**Rule: a commit message ends at its body. No `Co-Authored-By`, no `Signed-off-by`,
no model or tool credit of any kind.** `git log --format=%B -5` shows the trailer on
older commits, so copying the house style from history reproduces this mistake on
purpose — history is the wrong reference for the trailer, and right for everything
else (the `fix(hub):` scope prefix and the why-first body are both worth matching).

## Long-running bench commands must run detached — the 30 s tool cap is real

**Rule: anything that takes over 30 s MUST be launched in the background and
polled, never run inline.** A command that runs past ~30 s is killed by the tool
timeout *mid-flight*, which for these operations is not just an annoyance — a
killed WiFi OTA leaves a half-written inactive slot, and you pay the wait twice
(kill, then re-issue). The reliable idiom:

```
# write the work into /tmp/foo.sh, then launch fully detached and return now:
setsid /tmp/foo.sh </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 & disown
# ...the script itself appends "EXIT=$?" / "DONE $(date +%T)" to a /tmp log,
# and you poll that log with short `sleep N; cat/grep <log>` calls.
```

Two launch gotchas that burned time this session, both avoided by using a
**script file** rather than an inline one-liner:
- `nohup curl … &` alone still blocks the tool call — you must `setsid … &
  disown` AND redirect stdin/stdout/stderr (`</dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1`) for it
  to actually detach and return immediately.
- Nested double-quotes inside an inline `bash -c '…'` launcher break the shell.
  Put the payload in a file instead of quoting it around.
- Each command in a `run_commands` array is its **own shell**; a `cd` in one
  element does not carry to the next. Prefix every element with its own `cd`,
  or use absolute paths.

### Known operations that exceed 30 s (run these detached)
- **WiFi OTA push**: `curl --data-binary @build/gorb-os.bin
  http://<ip>/api/v1/ota` — ~40 s for the ~1.3 MB app over a healthy link, and
  it **aborts mid-stream** ("upload aborted by client") when rssi is marginal
  (< ~-70, ICMP loss). Prefer **serial flash** for the app on a flaky bench
  (`/tmp/serflash.sh` does app+otadata to ota_0 in ~25 s, hashes verified); use
  WiFi OTA only when the device is near its AP. The device keeps booting the old
  slot during a push, so it looks "silent" — that is the httpd worker blocked in
  the flash write, not a hang; poll, don't re-push (a second push corrupts).
- **Serial flash of a bulk image**: any `esptool write_flash` / `idf.py flash`
  that sends the app (1.3 MB) — and especially anything that re-sends
  `assets/clock_assets.bin` (~1.85 MB) — runs 25–40 s+. Detach and poll
  `/tmp/serflash-<ORB_LABEL>.log` (per orb) for `Hash of data verified` + `EXIT=0`.
- **`idf.py build` that recompiles many objects**: the steady-state rebuild of
  one file (`orb_net.c`) is ~10 s and is fine inline, but the **first build into
  a fresh `-B` dir**, or any build after an `sdkconfig`/Kconfig change that
  drags in mbedtls/LWIP, exceeds 30 s. Run detached and grep the log for
  `Project build complete` / `binary size` / `warning:` / `error:`.
- **A chip wipe, headless only — `orbflash -mode wipe`.** The hub's
  `POST /api/wipe` door and its Wipe chip button are gone (2026-10-15: the page
  offers lonely and nothing more destructive; a blank chip boot-loops and reads
  as a broken cable). The 16 MB ROM erase itself is tens of
  seconds, and the blank-check digest that follows costs about a minute more on
  the wire, so the whole operation is a couple of minutes with nothing printed
  during it. Run detached.
- **`idf.py flash monitor`**, and any `monitor`/serial capture held open longer
  than ~25 s (e.g. watching the association ladder). Use the short bounded
  capture scripts (`tools/capf.py <secs> <log> [--no-reset]`,
  `tools/cap.py <secs> [reset]`) with a duration under ~12 s inline; longer
  captures go detached.
- **Never park a port-opener (`cat /dev/ttyACM*`, `tail -f`, an open monitor)
  on an orb port to watch for a WSL reattach — detect the NODE, never open it.**
  Reattach detection is legitimate: when Windows retakes the port, HTTP and the
  hub go blind together. It needs no open: the device symlink's existence
  (`[ -e /dev/serial/by-id/…<MAC>…-if00 ]`) is the whole event, and
  `tools/orb-manager/scripts/wsl-usb.sh` already automates it
  (`node_of`/`wait_node` stat only; `watch` runs the Windows-side usbipd
  auto-attacher). A previous session instead left an inline
  `while :; do cat /dev/ttyACM0 >>/tmp/con.log; sleep 0.4; done`: every open
  pulsed DTR/RTS, its log recorded 62 USB_UART_CHIP_RESETs in one day, 25 into
  DOWNLOAD mode (board answers nothing — looks bricked), and its reopens
  wedged Orb Manager's identify op past its deadline and stranded the chip at
  "waiting for download". Console viewing goes through the hub's read-only
  USB console or a bounded `capf.py`, never a holder.

## Wiping an orb: the ROM has no chip-erase opcode, and `FLASH_MD5` needs a preamble

Measured on two orbs, 2026-10-14, while implementing `esprom.Wipe`.

- **There is no ROM chip erase.** esptool marks both `erase_flash` (0xD0) and
  `erase_region` (0xD1) `@stub_function_only`, so a bare S3 ROM rejects them and
  this project uploads no stub. The ROM's genuine erase is `FLASH_BEGIN`, which
  erases its whole range before accepting any data: `num_blocks=0` writes nothing,
  so offset 0 with `erase_size` = capacity **is** the whole-chip erase. That is the
  same request `EraseRegion` already sends for the factory reset, one bigger number.
- **`FLASH_MD5` (0x13) is a ROM command, but only after `SPI_SET_PARAMS`.** Attach
  and hash without configuring the geometry and the ROM answers status `[1,9]`,
  which looks exactly like an unsupported opcode and is not. esptool's no-stub
  `verify_flash` prints "Enabling default SPI flash mode" then "Configuring flash
  size" for precisely this reason. The reply layout is unusual too: 32 hex chars
  FIRST, status bytes after them, so `checkCommand` cannot parse this one response.
- **An erase is proven, not believed.** An erased flash reads all `0xFF`, so the
  check is `md5(0xFF × capacity)`; for the orb's 16 MB that is
  `d5929b7a5ad99f511fc83d7d0b48b85f`. A wipe that cannot show that digest returns
  an error rather than a success.
- **A wiped orb is dark, not bricked.** `/api/ports` still lists the port (the host
  reads the USB descriptors, and the `44:BD:8D:…` serial number is the burned eFuse
  MAC) with `probed:false` and no identity. Nothing prints, no AP opens, nothing
  announces. A hub-driven ending — the lonely write — also drops the hub's own
  memory of that board — fleet row,
  cached name, address grant — so **`/api/fleet` showing one board fewer is the
  expected result, not a lost row.** (A CLI `-mode wipe` leaves the hub's memory
  alone: it is a bench act, and the hub re-reads the port dark on its next sweep.)
  A board with nothing on it reports
  "no gorb-os firmware booted" and the `Upgrade needed` verdict. Flash it back.
- **A blank flash makes an S3 boot-loop, and the flapping port is the expected post-wipe
  state — not a fault.** Descriptors and the `44:BD:8D:…` eFuse serial come from ROM
  silicon, so a blank chip is still visible; but with no valid image the ROM bootloader
  reloads every boot, so the port comes and goes every few seconds. Measured on both
  wiped orbs (2026-10-14): enumerated as `303a:1001`, `cdc_acm` bound the ACM device,
  then `vhci_hcd: connection closed` about 1–3 s later, repeating forever. The symptom
  mimics a bad link exactly, so stop here before blaming cable, hub, rail or usbip.
- **Recover a blank board with the manual BOOT-to-download sequence.** Hold BOOT, tap
  and release RESET, keep holding BOOT a second, then release. That drops the ROM into
  the download mode instead of the boot loop, the port stops flapping, and the hub can
  talk to it. A **wipe should warn the operator to expect this**; a freshly wiped board
  is unreachable by software until someone presses the buttons.
- **`/api/lonely` — "Leave it lonely" — is the button-press-free exit from that state,
  and the standing answer to "what does a de-orbed board show?"** (requested verbally
  2026-10-14, first written down here after a session lost it — if this entry ever
  contradicts your memory of the design discussion, THIS was the decision.) An orb-manager
  build embeds `tools/orb-manager/lonely/lonely.bin`, a standalone ESP-IDF app: it boots,
  bounces a message block reading "I used to be an Orb, now I'm just lonely. Flash me!"
  and an 8-ball around the round CO5300 panel, and does nothing else — no WiFi, no NVS,
  no mDNS, no serial command door. `POST /api/lonely` (payload word `LONELY` — the page
  asks for no typed confirmation, the hub still requires the field; same
  streaming/registry/cancel contract the wipe door once had) writes it via
  `esprom.LonelyPlan`:
  the ACTIVE firmware bundle's bootloader @0x0, partition table @0x8000 and initial
  otadata @0x11000, and the lonely app @0x20000 (ota_0) — deliberately the orb's own
  geometry, so a New Orb flash over a lonely board replaces every byte this writes.
  A whole-flash `.bin` picked as the active firmware closes this door by name: all
  three borrowed images are inside the blob, fused into one span at 0x0 with no
  manifest naming any of them, so `Bundle.LonelyPlan` refuses with the shape and
  points at a `.zip` (`TestMergedBlobClosesTheLonelyDoor`).
  Works on a boot-looping blank board (`EnterDownloadMode`'s auto-reset wins the timing
  race the same way a New Orb flash does) and on a still-running orb. On success it
  forgets the board exactly like a wipe used to, **and marks the chip lonely** — the
  lonely app still enumerates the eFuse MAC over the same USB-JTAG descriptor, so an
  unmarked hub re-remembers the port on the next sweep and the row the operator ended
  rebuilds itself (the 2026-10-15 complaint: "it still keeps reporting as an orb").
  `endAsLonely` marks the chip **before** `forgetWipedOrb`, because that forget is what
  saves the state file: a mark landing after it never reaches disk, and the next launch
  lets the sweep rebuild the row the operator ended. `endaslonely_test.go` drives the pair
  and reads the file; `lonely_test.go` pins the call order.
  While a mark stands the board is invisible to the fleet, `fleetKnown` reports it
  unknown (New Orb shows a blank to adopt), the port list labels it `left lonely`, and
  the mark is cleared only by orb evidence: an orb flash's eFuse MAC, a serial-door ack,
  or an announcement. Marks persist in state.json. The board then enumerates steadily as
  `303a:1001` (USB-Serial/JTAG console, single enumeration, no reboot loop).
  The write also **erases the owner's settings**: the same ROM session wipes the NVS and
  storage regions read out of the ACTIVE bundle's own partition table (`esprom.NvsRegions`,
  the factory-reset-flash rule — never constants), and the app itself re-erases both
  partitions at every boot so a lonely board keeps nothing however it got there. The
  animation is composited — each dirty band is rebuilt background+message+ball and
  flushed once, never erase-then-draw — which is what the 2026-10-15 flicker complaint
  bought. Rebuild it with `tools/orb-manager/lonely/build.sh`
  (ESP-IDF v5.4 at `~/esp/esp-idf`; run it with bash, `export.sh` is not POSIX sh;
  `main/CMakeLists.txt` carries `PRIV_REQUIRES nvs_flash esp_lcd espressif__esp_lcd_co5300`
  — an explicit requirements list is exhaustive, so the display stack must be named).
- **An address is the whole qualification for the fleet list and the state file
  (2026-10-15).** The operator's words: "the only reason to keep an orb when it's not
  actively connected is to be able to ping its IP address. One that is not pingable does
  not matter, we can't manage it ever unless it connects to the serial port at which
  point it will self discover anyway." So `Snapshot` and `Restore` in `internal/orbfleet`
  carry only rows with `IP != ""`, and the roster's fleet tier skips address-less rows
  and lonely-marked ones. The `Remembered` flag no longer buys a line in the file — it is
  in-memory only, and `storeProvesOrb` still reads it to tell a known orb's cable from a
  blank board awaiting New Orb. This retired the earlier "flashed or named persists
  without an address" exception, which is what left an unplugged board and a
  just-ended lonely one sitting on the card. `remembered_test.go` pins the two halves of
  the rule — no address and no cable produces nothing, an address still holds its row —
  and `endaslonely_test.go` pins that one lonely save carries both the forget and the
  mark. A stale entry an older hub wrote dies on the
  next launch rather than being migrated.
- **Naming on the hub page is display-only, and a live Forget is a dialog, not a
  dead button (2026-10-15).** The hub shows the name the board answers to; it edits
  nothing — renames happen on the orb's own network page (`POST /api/v1/name`), over
  the serial `NAME` door, or at flash time in New Orb. When the Forget gate refuses a
  live row, `annotateForget` also sets `liveForget` + `livePort`, the card lights
  Forget, and the dialog offers the two endings that actually reach a live board,
  both over the USB lead, in this order: **Reset settings** (`/api/orb/cmd`
  `RESET ALL`; the
  ack write-back renames the store's row to match during the gap before the board
  answers again: on a clean erase the firmware seeds the default name **"The Orb"**
  into the fresh NVS itself (net_factory_reset), so the device and the card agree
  from the first poll; the next owner's word corrects it exactly like any device
  name does) and **Reset this orb → Firmware** (was "Erase flash completely"; the `/api/lonely` write, `LONELY` payload
  word, which erases the owner data and writes a minimal base image that holds the
  port for an easy reflash). The two visible labels never mention the internal
  `lonely` name or the base image's on-screen message — that is an inside joke; keep
  it out of every operator-facing string on the page, in the streamed log, and in the
  button labels. The internal door, embedded file and payload word keep the name). The whole-chip erase is not offered by the hub in any surface: the page
  lost the Wipe chip button and the route lost `/api/wipe`; `orbflash -mode wipe`
  remains the headless bench door. A test pins all of this (`ui_test.go`,
  `TestPageNeverOffersChipEraseAndTheNameIsDisplayOnly`).
  Neither ending asks for a typed word (2026-10-15: "the type to confirm is super
  annoying"): the button press is the confirmation, and reaching it already took
  pressing Forget and naming the ending. The card's Lonely confirm button is enabled the
  moment it is armed, and `#ob-lonely-word` is gone from the page. The hub's payload gate
  stays, so a scripted or curl caller still has to send `LONELY` / `RESET ALL`.
  `TestLonelyPageHasTheDoorAndNoTypedWord` pins the ceremony's absence both ways.
- **One MAC, two spellings — key on the canonical form inside the collection.**
  `orbmdns.CanonicalMAC` returns bare lowercase hex and is what the cable sweep, the
  fleet store and the roster key on; `macNorm` returns colon-separated pairs, which is
  also how the USB descriptor and the flash's eFuse read print it. The lonely set first
  stored whatever its caller handed it, so a mark written in pairs answered only pair
  queries: the port list called the chip lonely while the sweep rebuilt its fleet row
  nameless beside it. Measured on the bench, 2026-10-15. Normalize at the collection
  boundary, never at each call site, and load old state files through the same normalizer.
- **The board owns its name, so the hub needs a read path — and the boot banner is
  the only one.** The serial door writes `NAME` and never reads it, and the status fold
  needs an address to dial, so a station board on a lead with no lease was unreadable:
  a hub that had never seen it online showed a blank card for a board somebody had
  already named. `orb_net_start` now prints `GorbOS-NAME <name>` after the state banner,
  raw to end of line the way `PROVSCAN` prints an SSID (a name may hold a space, so a
  `\S+` class drops it), and only when named — an unnamed board prints nothing, and the
  hub must read silence as no news rather than as a cleared name, or it wipes the cache
  of every board on older firmware. `esprom.ParseOrbName` is the parse; `foldBootName`
  is the write, and it updates a row the hub already holds rather than creating one.
  `bootname_test.go` pins the pair from both sides by rendering the firmware's own
  format string and feeding the result to the parse. Measured on the bench, 2026-10-15.
- **The join dialog opens LOCKED and unlocks only on a verdict — and its zone picker
  is `/api/tz`'s rows, not strings.** The flash's boot window (default now 28 s, must
  outlast `SCAN_TIMEOUT_US`) can close before the firmware's boot scan prints its
  PROVSCAN rows; `/api/watch` then reports the AP with an empty list, and a form left
  open there is the dead-empty picker this replaced. The dialog now carries a spinner
  until a verdict lands, auto-drives one serial `SCAN` when the captured list is empty
  (`/api/orb/cmd`, works in every state including provisioning), and keeps a manual
  **Rescan** beside it. `loadZones` must unwrap the `{ok,data}` envelope and consume
  rows as `{name,posix,iana,label}` objects — reading `d.zones` or treating a row as a
  string renders a dead-empty picker with no error anywhere; "Use my location" scores
  each row's January and July `Intl` offsets against the longitude's solar line.
  `tools/orb-manager/webui_test.go` pins every `$("#id")` in `app.js` against the
  markup, because a missing `#wj-lock` throws inside `joinLock` and locks the dialog
- **An empty picker after a fresh flash was the argument trap, not the firmware.** The
  same symptom had a second, likelier cause: `openJoinDialog(port, mac, skipAp)` took the
  orb's NAME as its third argument at the New Orb call site (`web/app.js`), a truthy
  string, so `/api/watch` ran with `skip_ap` set and Phase A — the only phase holding the
  AP verdict and the boot scan — never ran. Fresh flashes answered `mode:"no_ip"` with an
  empty picker and the dialog said the orb had come up with nothing. Pass flags as flags.
  `TestJoinDialogIsNeverOpenedWithANameInTheSkipFlag` in `ui_test.go` pins every call site
  (negative control run against the old call). A `no_ip`/`unknown` verdict with a cable
  bound is not a dead end either: the serial door answers in every state, so that branch
  now re-drives the scan instead of pointing the operator at an address nobody has. And a
  locked dialog is never silent: `watchLog` mirrors the newest hub line onto the lock
  sentence, and `/api/watch` Phase B emits one per poll tick, because a 45 s wait behind a
  fixed "rebooting into its new firmware" sentence reads as a hang (bench, 2026-10-15).
- **Never conclude "the page never called that door" from a hub log.** The access line is
  written AFTER `ServeHTTP` returns, so an in-flight streaming call has no line yet; and a
  hub started from a shell writes its lines to that terminal, not to whatever `/tmp/hub*.log`
  an agent redirected earlier. Trust the log only after proving it is the live process's
  stdout (`ps` the pid, check the file's mtime against the request). Counting routes in a
  stale file sent one wrong diagnosis down this path (2026-10-15).
  open forever.
- **Idle presence is not a health test, and neither is a DTR/RTS drop.** A boot-looping
  orb reads `present=True status=OK` on the host between reloads, and asserting DTR/RTS
  drives the auto-reset circuit so the handle dies inside ~2 s on a good board. Clear a
  board of hardware by forcing download mode and seeing it hold. `vhci_hcd: connection
  closed` only says the host tore the session down; it names neither device nor cause.

### Known operations that are SAFE inline (under 30 s)
- `python3 tools/host_tz_test.py` (~1 s), `python3 tools/host_sd_test.py` (~2 s),
  `python3 tools/host_mdns_test.py` (~4 s, and ~4 min with `--mutations`).
- Steady-state `idf.py build` after editing a single source file (~10 s).
- `esptool erase_region` / a status `curl` / `ping` / `nm`+`strings` on an elf.
- Short serial captures (≤ ~12 s) like `tools/capf.py 8 /tmp/x.txt --no-reset`.

## `version.txt` is tracked, `PROJECT_VER` is cached — a bump with no reconfigure stamps the old version

`tools/orb_version.sh` composes the stamp (`v1.0.0+92424ff-dirty`) and root
`CMakeLists.txt` runs it at **configure** time. `idf.py build` alone recompiles changed
sources and keeps the cached stamp, so an image carrying new code can print an old
version. The fix after any `version.txt` edit is `idf.py reconfigure && idf.py build`.
`tools/serflash.sh` reads the stamp back out of `build/gorb-os.bin` and refuses the
flash on a mismatch, so the failure is loud — but only at flash time. `idf.py
set-version` does not help: it re-runs IDF's own finder, which never sees `version.txt`
once `PROJECT_VER` is set. Run `tools/orb_version.sh --self-test` (~2 s, no toolchain)
after touching the composer; the hub's Go suite does not run it.

## sdkconfig is local + untracked — a NEW Kconfig `default y` does NOT appear on a rebuild

Only `sdkconfig` is untracked here (there is no `*_defconfig`; `sdkconfig.defaults`
IS tracked, and a new option needs a line in it for a fresh clone to get it).
`idf.py build` treats an existing `sdkconfig` as authoritative:
adding a `config FOO` with `default y` to `main/Kconfig` does **not** materialize
`FOO=y` into the existing `sdkconfig`/`build/config/sdkconfig.h` — so
`#if CONFIG_FOO` silently compiles the feature **out**, and the only tell is a
binary that barely grows. This bit `CONFIG_ORB_NET_CAPTIVE_DNS`: the first "clean
build" was a false pass (+96 B = just the URI strings, not the task).

Therefore, **whenever you add a Kconfig symbol and want it on, prove it actually
compiled in — do not trust `EXIT=0`:**
1. grep `build/config/sdkconfig.h` for `#define CONFIG_<SYM> 1` (absent = out);
2. `xtensa-esp32s3-elf-nm build/gorb-os.elf | grep <new_fn>` and
   `strings … | grep <new log line>`;
3. sanity-check the `binary size` delta is the right order of magnitude.
Fix a missing symbol by writing the explicit `CONFIG_<SYM>=y` line into
`sdkconfig` (append if absent), then rebuild. Fresh clones get `default y` for
free; it's the *existing* local `sdkconfig` that silently excludes it.

## The config pages are C strings, and a JS syntax error in them is invisible on a radio-less bench

There are two pages, each one giant `static const char[]` of concatenated
`"…"` fragments: `HTML_LANDING` (provisioning at `/`) and `HTML_TZ` (the clock
at `/timezone`). The timezone UI lives only on the clock page — the owner took
it off the network page outright after the first phone tests. The clock page
renders city rows from `/api/v1/config` (`label` + `iana` columns), reads the
phone's zone via `Intl…resolvedOptions()` and auto-applies it; for a zone the
table does not carry it SYNTHESIZES the DST rule client-side from the phone's
own IANA database (bisection of monthly offset windows, refused when the
pattern moves between years — Jerusalem/Casablanca class), and every set is
verified by comparing the phone's offset against the device's measured
`tz_offset` echo. It shows no POSIX rules to a person, ever — the rules are
built in the browser and posted, never displayed. The zone table itself
(`s_tz_tbl`) is machine-generated from the IANA zone of the same name and the
host suite re-proves every row against the machine's tzdata (2025-2028, every
noon + the minute either side of every transition); regenerate rows via
`tools/host_tz_test.py`'s derivation, do NOT hand-edit a rule, and key new
rows on the canonical IANA name (the five `US/*` keys are frozen because NVS
may already store them). `tools/host_tz_test.py` parses and pins both pages
and enforces those boundaries by forbidding tz UI on the landing page and
`posix`/<select> on the clock page. Three ways the pages break in a
way curl + a working server can hide:

1. **Unbalanced parens across fragment boundaries.** Each C line looks fine in
   isolation; only the compiler's concatenation makes `say(a+(b?'x':'')+(c?'y':''))`
   wrong, and a phone renders the result as a page stuck at "loading…" with an
   empty network list AND an empty timezone dropdown — because the first parse
   error kills the entire `<script>`, so `status()`, `scan()` and `tzload()` all
   silently never run. That empty-dropdown symptom (reported from a real phone)
   was exactly this: one extra `)`. Reviewing source line-by-line shows nothing.
2. **A blocking handler starving the page's parallel requests.** See the
   `NET_CMD_SCAN`/`scan_collect` code: the original ran
   `esp_wifi_scan_start(block=true)` on httpd's single worker, so the status poll
   and the tz fetch queued behind a 5-10 s scan. Sequential curl never shows it;
   a browser fires its first requests at once.
3. **A call to a function whose definition was deleted.** Syntax-clean, every
   substring pin green, and the button just does nothing on the phone. This
   happened for real while writing the synthesis engine (a rewrite dropped
   `gmtRule`'s definition but left its call). The host suite lints both pages
   for calls to functions that do not exist — keep that lint green too.

`tools/host_tz_test.py` now rebuilds the page bytes exactly as the compiler does
(concatenate the fragments, C-unescape) and parses the `<script>` with esprima —
a syntax error fails the host suite. This is the file's own rule in action: what
the no-radio bench can't observe must be lifted to a host check. **Keep that check
green whenever you touch the page.** (Verified by mutation: re-injecting the `)`
turns it red.) esprima is not stdlib — a missing parser is a loud SKIP, not a
pass, so `pip install esprima` if it skips. The suite is self-verifying:
`python3 tools/host_tz_test.py --mutations` re-runs it against fourteen
deliberate regressions (deleted synth engine, rubber-stamped echo check,
hand-edited rule, shrunk buffer, …) and every one must fail it with a named
verdict; a battery that cannot fail is not a test.

## Simulating the phone side of provisioning on the radio-less bench

The host has no WiFi radio, so "a phone joins orb-xxxxxx" is the one link the
bench cannot supply — but everything AFTER the first join is testable as a
station: once a phone has provisioned the orb onto a network the bench can
also reach (the home LAN), the orb boots connected, its LAN IP answers curl,
and APSTA lets tooling open the AP (`POST /api/v1/wifi/ap {}` — the config
page has no button for this, see the h_wifi_ap comment) while the station
stays up. That exercises the full page, the connected-ack AP close (watch
serial for `SoftAP down (lease held, hold expired)`), and captive DNS.
Credentials SURVIVE app re-flashes — only `erase_region 0x9000` (nvs) +
`0xD60000` (storage) removes them — so keep one bench orb provisioned and
re-wipe only to test first-boot provisioning itself.
(Older notes mentioned `www/provisioner.html.gz` and `WIFI_SSID` baked from
`.env` at build time: that mechanism is NOT in this tree — provisioning is
entirely `HTML_LANDING` in `orb_net.c`. Verify before repeating that claim.)

## Bench has no WiFi radio — verify what you can, and say what you can't

There is no wireless adapter on the host, so the phone-side captive-portal popup
("Sign in to network") and any real client joining the AP cannot be proven here.
What IS verifiable on the bench, via USB serial + the LAN the orb joins as a
station:
## One clone, one orb — the .orb-target binding (2026-10-11)

Two ESP32-S3 orbs share this bench and enumerate identically (`303a:1001`); only
the MAC in the `/dev/serial/by-id` name tells them apart. Each clone therefore
binds exactly one orb through the **untracked** `.orb-target` at its root
(`ORB_LABEL`, `ORB_BASE_MAC`, `ORB_SERIAL`, `ORB_IP`), and every device tool in
`tools/` refuses to run without it: no bare `/dev/ttyACMx` and no hardcoded orb
IP survives in tracked code. `tools/orb_bind.sh` (shell) and `tools/orb_bind.py`
(python) implement the gates — port must exist, esptool `read_mac` must match
the bound MAC, `ip neigh` must confirm the LAN address — and the per-orb
sidecar flock in `/tmp/orb-<label>-<mac>.lock` makes a second writer fail
loudly. Binding follows the working copy, not the branch: switching branches
never moves a tool onto the other orb. Current bindings: clone `gorb-os-2` →
orb-A (`44:bd:8d:60:80:88`, ttyACM0, `192.168.2.32`); clone `gorb-os` → orb-B
(`44:bd:8d:78:26:18`, ttyACM1, `192.168.2.34`). Both orbs are provisioned and
self-join GTROUTER2 at boot (~2.5 s observed 2026-10-11); neither runs an AP
unless opened via `/api/v1/wifi/ap`. If a DHCP lease moves, boot-capture to
read the new address and fix `ORB_IP` there — the ip-neigh gate re-proves it

- **Address the clone's `ORB_IP`, never an IP carried in session memory.**
  2026-10-25: a full share-card bench cycle ran against `192.168.2.32` —
  orb-A — from a `gorb-os` shell whose binding line is two paragraphs above.
  The cycle was valid evidence for Bronze and a protocol violation for the
  clone doing the editing; the owner caught it. Read `.orb-target` at the
  start of every device touch.
- **`/api/orb/update` refuses a rebuild of the same semver head.** The gate
  compares `v1.1.0+5af35cf-dirty` to `v1.1.0+4ba2bde` as `same-version` — the
  commit suffix is metadata to `firmware.Compare`. A new build on an orb
  already at `v1.1.0+<anything>` needs `"force":true`; the push itself stays
  honest (it re-verifies the device's stamp on return).
every run. If usbipd re-attach renames ACM numbers, nothing breaks: tools ask
by by-id path, not number.

- Serial console: plain opens work (user is in `dialout`; re-attach via usbipd
  can drop it — `sudo` then). Flash/boot capture: `tools/capf.py <secs> <log>
  [--no-reset]`, `tools/cap.py <secs> [reset]`, `tools/grab_boot.py`.
- `/api/v1/status` over LAN (state/fw/slot/img/rssi/synced/ap/assoc_fails).
- AP open/close lifecycle + `captive DNS answering for …` via
  `tools/ap_cp_test.sh` (opens AP while station holds a lease, checks the station
  stays connected+synced, fires the probe endpoints, closes AP, greps serial).
- **Audit loop**: flash, then confirm `/api/v1/status`'s `fw` is the git hash you
  built (`-dirty` = uncommitted edits). Ship = test = commit, same bytes.
  **Caveat, measured 2026-10-11: that check can pass while lying.** `fw` comes from
  `esp_app_desc_t`, which CMake stamps at *configure* time and caches; `idf.py build`
  recompiles changed sources but keeps the old stamp. A tree with uncommitted edits
  therefore flashed an image reporting a clean hash it was never built from (observed:
  `art_bank.c` edited 14:46:10, image built 14:46:31, stamp still plain `54094e8`).
  Trust the stamp only after `idf.py reconfigure && idf.py build`, and rely on
  `tools/serflash.sh`, which now reads the version out of the `.bin` itself
  (`esp_app_desc_t.version`, byte 48), compares it to `git describe --always --dirty`,
  and exits 1 without flashing on a mismatch. The verdict goes to
  `/tmp/serflash-audit-<ORB_LABEL>.log` (per orb, so the two clones' audits
  never overwrite each other); `/tmp/serflash.sh` is a wrapper for it.
- **A green flash audit proves bytes, not bootability.** The stamp audit checks
  the image identity; it cannot see a budget. After the FIRST flash of any build
  that adds static arrays of ~1 KB or more, serial-capture the boot and grep
  `heap_init`, `post-httpd_start`, `httpd_start:` and `Failed to allocate` before
  trusting anything else. P5.5 is the named lesson (`docs/app-platform-plan.md`
  §16): 16 embedded 920 B layer tables = 14,720 B of `.bss`, host suite + compile +
  link all green, and the board answered `httpd_start: ESP_ERR_HTTPD_TASK` — the
  config server died while the dial kept drawing. The `(check LWIP_MAX_SOCKETS…)`
  hint lies at this heap level; `internal free`/`largest` on the boot line is the
  verdict. A new static table needs its sizeof quoted in the commit message.
- **A 200 header is not a response.** A marginal radio can complete TLS-less
  request headers in ~0.2 s and deliver zero body bytes for a minute: the bench's
  2026-10-14 session saw `code=200 size=0` at 12-25 s on three routes while ping
  answered at 30 ms. Any HTTP-dependent measurement must demand transferred bytes
  (`curl -w '%{size_download}'` > 0, body to a file) before it trusts a reading,
  and a bench day whose boot log shows `esp-aes: Failed to allocate memory` or
  several BSSIDs across retries (band steering eating the ~2 KB DMA pool) is a day
  the *large-body* routes do not exist — the same board transferred 50-63 B bodies
  in well under a second all evening while the ~1 KB status body stalled, so
  trigger-and-small-response steps stay usable and big-body reads go to serial.
- **A queued 200 is not a job result, and a starved boot line is not physical
  state.** `POST /api/v1/sd/install` answers `200 {"state":"installing"}` for an
  id that is nowhere on the card; the worker then logs the truth (`install: no
  such theme on the card`, `job done`) seconds later. Read the worker's serial
  line, not the queue's. Same family: a boot-time `send_cid 0x101` triple is the
  DMA budget at that instant, not an empty slot — the retry-on-demand route is
  the witness for physical state. §16.1 is the named lesson: both misreadings
  happened in one session and each produced a wrong blocker in the record.

Anything needing a radio to *observe* (the popup, real DNS answer to a client)
belongs in `tools/host_tz_test.py` by lifting the pure logic verbatim — see how
`dns_build_reply`, `retry_delay_s`, `sta_leave_is_self_inflicted` and the tz
block are tested. Do not claim a radio-dependent behavior is verified.
## Clock themes from watch-designer kits (tourbillon/animated second hand)

`tools/bake_clock.py` takes a layered watch kit: base gear frames, hands/,
layers/{dial,glass,outer}, an optional tourbillon/, and a manifest.json carrying
every pivot/centre. The only kit the repo still ships is
`assets/sources/av-kit/watch-aviator-kit.zip`, the source of the embedded bank;
it has no tourbillon. Do NOT trust the manifest's tourbillon `centre`: it is the
renderer's own claim and was found wrong by half a texel on both axes (kit2:
declared [233,233], measured centroid (232.5,232.5) — a texel corner no integer
pivot can sit on, giving a 1.70 px hub bob). Run
`python3 tools/kit_check.py <kit.zip>` on the zip BEFORE baking and let it gate
the drop; see `docs/tourbillon-axis-spec.md`. The gate needs a kit WITH a
tourbillon, so it takes an exported kit, not the shipped av-kit. After the
bake, `python3 tools/sim_tourb_bob.py` re-measures on the emitted pair. The three
tools share one implementation (sim_tourb_mark's integer maths, kit_check's red
gate), so they cannot disagree.
The bake itself emits `assets/clock_assets.{bin,h}` as one pair; they are
generated together and must be committed together — the firmware reads
`clk_theme` by layout, so a stale half of the pair is a corrupt dial.
System python has no PIL; use a venv with `pillow numpy` for both tools.

    /tmp/bakevenv/bin/python tools/prep_watch_kit.py <kit.zip> /tmp/out
    /tmp/bakevenv/bin/python tools/bake_clock.py /tmp/out/theme \
        --bg-frames /tmp/out/frames --bg-fps 15 \
        --tourbillon /tmp/out/tourbillon --tourbillon-fps 15 \
        --force-sweep --tick-rate 15 --dump-dir /tmp/preview


The hub's SHIPPED DEFAULT THEME is **Skeleton**
(`tools/orb-manager/themes/skeleton.orbtheme`), and it is a different artifact from the
compiled-in bank above, reaching the dial through a different door. `installTheme` seeds the
first `.orbtheme` the resolved `themes/` directory holds, so that one file IS the theme a
wiped board gets. It is a Composer bake (FORMAT 3, abi 4) carrying a class 9 preview plane,
and that plane is exactly why it replaced the Aviator seed: `tools/make_seed_theme.py`
(deleted) lifted the seed out of `assets/orb_default.img`, a slot image carries no preview
plane, and so the shipped default answered `/api/v1/sd/preview` with 404 while the Themes
card drew an empty frame beside its own name. `bake_clock.py` cannot close that gap — it
refuses class 9 by design — so the ONLY way to bake a default is the Composer. Re-bake with
`_selftest/bank3-drive.html?dial=skeleton&name=Skeleton` on Windows Chrome (deploy first with
`deploy_test_folder.py`; Chrome cannot read the `\\wsl.localhost` UNC path), lift the `B64:`
line out of the dumped `<pre id="out">`, then `python3 tools/make_default_theme.py <bake>` —
it refuses a bake with no preview row, a name that disagrees with its own filename, or an abi
the firmware will not read. Never hand-edit the container. The sentinel leg in
`tools/host_orbapp_install_test.py` runs `make_default_theme.py --check`, which needs no
browser. A re-bake is NOT byte-identical to the committed file and must not be expected to
be: 7 bytes move — the `baked_ms` wall-clock stamp at header word 12 and the header CRC over
it at word 28 — while art CRC, geometry CRC, serial and name all match. That is why the gate
walks the element table instead of hashing the file. The compiled-in pair is still av-kit,
and this changes nothing about it.

The SHIPPED compiled-in bank is **av-kit** (composer preset `Aviator`, 15 base
frames at 15 fps, no tourbillon), not kit3. Its source kit is committed at
`assets/sources/av-kit/` with its `DESIGN_ID`; read that folder's README before
re-baking. It reproduces exactly on the geometry half and to within 59 bytes
(0.005%, one 1-px antialiasing fringe at 932 B stride) on pixels — measured
2026-10-12 by running the recipe below against the committed zip. Verify rather
than believe: the emitted `.h` differs only in its provenance comment, which
records the input dir path, so compare the `.bin` by md5 and the `.h` by ignoring
line 4. `assets/clock_assets.h:3` names the source theme; if it disagrees with the
kit you just baked, you baked the wrong kit.

    /tmp/bakevenv/bin/python tools/prep_watch_kit.py assets/sources/av-kit/watch-aviator-kit.zip /tmp/p1/av-kit
    /tmp/bakevenv/bin/python tools/bake_clock.py /tmp/p1/av-kit/theme \
        --bg-frames /tmp/p1/av-kit/frames --bg-fps 15 --tick-rate 15 --force-sweep

The pair has a second door now (P6.5, §25.6): `tools/bake_clock.py --from-bundle
<bundle.orbtheme>` rebuilds `clock_assets.{bin,h}` from a FORMAT 3 section's element
table instead of from a kit, with no PIL in the loop. Both paths print through the one
`.h` writer in `_finish()`; only the provenance comment differs, so the same
compare-the-`.h`-ignoring-provenance rule applies to a rebuilt pair. The container is
that pair's source of truth, and anything the table cannot state (a tourbillon, a real
delta bank, an extent its catalogue does not account for) refuses with a sentence rather
than rebuilding a wrong clock. Sentinel: `tools/host_from_bundle_test.py` (76 checks, 18
refusal vectors) and `tools/mut_from_bundle.py` (11/11).

The 59-page **flagship** bench bank (`LAT_BYTES_KIT3 = 3858874` in
`main/art/art_bank.c:642`) was baked from `watch-orbital-kit3.zip`, sha256
c910be26dde63eb4... That zip and its prep output left the repo on 2026-10-12;
`git log --diff-filter=D -- assets/sources/` recovers them. The constant is a
pinned measurement of art no longer in the tree, so re-running that bake
requires the kit back from history first. `headless-orbtheme.test.js`'s
whole-container fixture now bakes the shipped av-kit instead: prep it with
`prep_watch_kit.py`, then bake with `--bg-fps 15 --tick-rate 15 --matte off
--bg-occlude auto`, which mirrors the JS side's manifest-derived cadence.

What kit3 measured, kept as the acceptance record: carriage
bbox=(109,46,249,310) pivot=(233,233) centre=(233,233), i.e. header
pivotx/pivoty = (124,187), 0.0000 px from the measured staff axis. kit2 was the
rejected case (0.707 px off-axis) and is likewise no longer committed.

Layer mapping (kit drawOrder -> compositor slots, verified against the ink radial
surveys — check the same numbers before trusting a new kit's art into a slot):
the gear base is the `--bg-frames` delta bank; the tourbillon is the SECOND-HAND
slot as a frame bank (`--tourbillon`, max 16 frames, per-frame diff rects chained
vs predecessor exactly like the delta bank); the dial is `static1` via
`hands.order=[2,3,0,1]` (over the carriage, under the hands); the glass sheen is
the single `clock_overlay.png`; the bezel is matted INTO the bank frames by
`prep_watch_kit.py` because its rim is fully opaque and no layer above reaches
r>=220 (minute hand tops out at r=199) — a runtime ring would cost PSRAM blends
the canvas restore serves for free. Glass must stay the overlay: the kit draws
hands UNDER glass, so folding it anywhere else is a fidelity loss.

**Bake-time static composite (`--matte`) — the two compositing methods.** A full-coverage
static layer (the dial disc, the glass) otherwise pays a `blend_px` on every commit across
the whole marked span. `tools/bake_clock.py --matte auto` folds such a layer into the
background it sits on (the plate, or every frame of the delta bank) so it costs *nothing*
at runtime, and records it in `clk_theme_t.matte_mask` (bit per hand slot, bit 5 = the
glass); `clock_view.c` then omits the layer from both the LVGL objects and the L4 hook's
job list, and the bake ships that sprite blank. `--matte off` keeps every layer a runtime
composite. Default is `auto`, and auto means PROVED, never assumed: a fold is legal iff
the layer never rotates, blends NORMAL, and its alpha is exactly 0 on every pixel any
LOWER layer can ever paint (a rotating hand can paint its whole ink annulus about its
centre — `_ink_reachable`). Alpha 255 does not license it either: matted, a lower layer
would paint *over* the opaque one instead of under it. Read the printed verdict before
changing the flag — on the orbital kit both statics are refused (dial: 64,151 of 96,406
ink px under the carriage's r=0..221 reach; glass: 112,267 of 113,422 px, max alpha 54),
so this art stays the runtime-composited type and `matte_mask` is 0. `--matte force`
overrides the proof for measurement only and prices the lie in pixels: it does reach
8-9 fps (avg commit 136-144 -> 82-94 ms, `dial 0/0 glass 0/0` in the blend telemetry,
1.24M -> 613k blends/s) but changes 20,109 px of the panel by up to 63 RGB565 steps —
the carriage loses the dial's tint and the hands lose the glass sheen. **A forced fold is
never a shippable setting**; if a theme needs the fps, the art must change so the proof
passes (the way the bezel rim does), not the proof. Fold nothing when `plateFollow` is
set — a baked-in layer would slide across the glass with the parallax. The fold composites
in the device's own RGB565 (`_mix565`), never PIL's 8-bit alpha_composite, so a proved
fold is bit-identical to the blend it replaces — verified by an `auto` bake reproducing
the shipped `clock_assets.bin` byte for byte.

Firmware rules this dial depends on (`main/clock/clock_view.c`):
- `tourbillon_advance()` swaps the shown frame off the wall clock (15 fps) and
  marks ONLY the union of the outgoing+incoming baked diff rects at the current
  angle; the orbit itself is plain hand rotation (manifest's seconds/60x360).
  First draw marks the full swept box. Park/resume resets `s_tourb_shown`.
- The sweeping carriage's orbit step marks the ROTATED INK, not the box:
  `tourbillon_ink_mark()` (from `apply_angles`, i==2 only, AND from
  `tourbillon_advance` for the INCOMING frame at the swap angle — the baked
  diff rects are chained against a neighbour frame, and a 15 fps bank on a
  ~7 fps tick often jumps two, so neither rect covers the g->f delta; the
  incoming chord stamp is what makes the swap complete) rotates every source
  row's ink chord of frame f — per-frame chords `s_tourb_cx0/1` are measured
  once off the alpha plane into PSRAM — into the `s_wp_*` device-row spans,
  then releases <=466 `mark_rows` calls. Both orbit poses are stamped (the
  vacated pixels are the other half of the job). Fallbacks that keep the box
  union: non-L4, `s_shadow[2]`, bank never composited, chord alloc failed.
  TWO stamp invariants, both invisible to the fps counter when broken:
  1. The forward rotation uses `rot_sin_cos()` — paint_layer's exact
     interpolated /1024 trig pair — scaled **\*64** into the `>> 16` products
     (1.0 == 65536). The first builds fed LVGL's /32768 `trigo_sin` table
     (or sinma\*32) straight into the `>> 16`: stamps landed at HALF radius,
     the carriage's outer ring was never restored/repainted, and it ghosted
     a full revolution while a crisp diff-box rectangle glared next to it.
  2. A chord whose rotated segment is WIDER than tall (|dx| > rows) stamps
     its full span on every row it (+rpad) touches — the row-walk, which is
     correct and tight for steep segments, marks only a ~7 px window per row
     there and leaked the counterweight tail near the 12 pose. rpad rows
     beyond a steep segment's ends extrapolate x by step16 (that IS covered).
  Verify any change to stamps or paint_layer with
  `python3 tools/sim_tourb_mark.py`: it ports both integer pipelines against
  the baked alpha and asserts every pixel either pose paints lies inside the
  tick's marked spans (dense at cardinal poses; exit 0 = clean).
- `mark_rows(y0,y1,x0,x1,force)`: rows are handed to the display through
  `display_mark_rows_clocked`; the GIF push marks force=true (its pixels
  reached mirror+canvas without the panel ever seeing them). The commit hook
  `clock_layer_commit` then FLUSH-GATES non-force rows: it snapshots each
  marked row, restores+re-paints, and `display_commit_row(y, memcmp)` sends
  only rows whose rebuild came out different (LVGL-chunk rows always ride,
  via `s_chunk_row`). Without this the disc of a sweep re-DMAed ~200k px of
  identical pixels per tick and capped fps at 5 even after the blends were
  cheap. `s_row_snap` alloc failure = every marked row flushes (safe legacy).
- `mark_all_layers()` early-returns when `clk_theme.tourbillon_n > 0`: with
  full-coverage art (translucent dial + glass over every row) the classic
  per-tick full-box re-mark measured 5 fps; letting only the movers mark their
  own rows (apply_angles / tourbillon_advance) runs 9-10 fps. Classic themes
  (tourbillon_n == 0) keep the old discipline verbatim — do not "optimise" it
  for them without re-reading the freeze-bug note above that function.
- `paint_layer()` has an identity fast path at ang % 360 == 0 (the dial and the
  glass never rotate); `blend_px()` is the single blend implementation both
  loops share — keep it that way.
- `CLK_GIF_THROTTLE 2` does nothing on this art (tick period exceeds the GIF
  deadline, so every tick is due anyway); measured no fps change. Leave at 1.

The kit's orbit SWEEPS: bake with `--force-sweep --tick-rate 15`. tickRate must be
>= the bank fps so `hand_beat_ticks()` yields one tick per beat (tpb<=1 = true
wall-clock sampling every tick); a low tickRate RE-QUANTISES the sweep to that
many beats/s and at rate 1 is indistinguishable from ticking. Sweep costs no
extra fps now (see the rotated-ink + flush-gate rules above): both the orbit
step's marks and the DMA are confined to the carriage's actual ink, so dial
and glass only re-blend where the carriage passed. Quantising to tpb=2
(`--tick-rate 7`) measured NO higher fps (pipeline already saturated) for
its steeper steps — rate 15 is the best sweep setting. Classic tick art
keeps the same discipline either way — sweep lives in the theme, not the code.

Steam-punk is NOT lost: `git checkout assets/clock_assets.bin assets/clock_assets.h
main/CMakeLists.txt --` restores the shipped 1.85 MB pair (it rebuilds identically
from the same --bg-gif orb_bg.gif command, and /tmp/steam-punk-assets-backup holds
a copy). Swap the pair, `idf.py build`, and the full `idf.py flash` (the assets
partition write is the slow ~3 min part — detach, per above).

Verified on the bench at fw with this theme: boot logs `tourbillon: 15 frames @
15 fps on the second-hand slot, orbit about (233,233)`; sweep steady 6-7 fps
(avg commit ~145 ms, px/s ~0.8-1.0 M, no Guru) with `tools/sim_tourb_mark.py`
CLEAN — every pixel either pose paints provably lies inside the tick's marks.
(The earlier "9 fps" figures were taken while the stamps ran at half radius:
unmarked pixels were neither restored nor re-blended, i.e. a third of the
work — and of the artifacts — was simply being skipped. Correct marks cost
what they cost; speed work must shave per-px blend cost, not coverage.)
Before the rotated-ink marks + commit flush gate, sweep ran 5 fps at ~450
rows/commit.
`--dump-dir` writes the bake's own inspection PNGs (canvas_NN, stale_NN must be
black, sprites over checkerboard). They are debug output, not inputs: point it
outside the repo, nothing reads them.

## The theme upload path, and what the no-card bench can prove about it

`main/sd/orb_sd.c` owns the card, the name/path validators, the write-to-temp
rename and the upload destination. `main/art/orb_theme.c` owns the container
header parser and the boot/load decisions. `main/net/orb_net.c`'s `/themes` page
owns the browser side. An SD socket empty and a radio off prove nothing about
that path, so `tools/host_sd_test.py` covers it on the host instead. It needs no
IDF, no radio and no card, and takes ~2 s. It lifts the shipped
functions verbatim from `orb_sd.c` / `orb_theme.c` / `orb_net.c`. Same rule as
`host_tz_test.py`: the real functions, not a re-typed copy. Its checks:

- **Name + path validation**, every rejection reason spelled out (traversal,
  embedded slash, leading dot, empty, over-long, all-dots, control bytes,
  over-ASCII), the built path compared byte for byte, the buffer boundary
  measured both ways (`outsz` that must fit and one byte less, with a canary
  tail that catches any write past it).
- **The geometry reader's refusals**, the shipped parser must reject each
  damaged-blob case with the *exact* reason the firmware emits. Non-NULL is not
  enough: the first draft of this suite let the tourbillon-extent mutation
  survive because a different downstream check caught the same blob for a
  different reason, and the vacated check stayed invisible.
- **The header reader**, real containers from `tools/orbtheme.py` plus
  deliberately damaged ones (bad magic, wrong format/version, wrong device,
  wrong panel size, implausible blob, oversized payload, torn header, trailing
  words). One known-answer row: a header copied out of the orbital-arrow-3 bake
  is embedded in the harness and must be ACCEPTED. A stub of `esp_rom_crc32_le`
  sits in the harness and must
  match the ROM's init/final inversion, that row is what proves the stub.
- **`/themes`' own `<script>`**, parsed with esprima (a missing parser is a
  loud SKIP, never a pass), every function it calls matched against the
  functions actually lifted out of the shipped `.c`, and each critical
  interaction pinned to a substring: the delete confirmation, the upload's
  `XMLHttpRequest` with `upload.onprogress` (a `fetch` cannot report upload
  progress), the 50 KB chunk, the `.part` cleanup in the `finally`.

- **Two source lints over the card code**, `chunk_lint()` and `lock_lint()`. See the
  next section: neither is a lift, both are regex over `_code_only()` output.

`python3 tools/host_sd_test.py --mutations` re-runs the whole suite against
24 deliberate regressions (validator loosened, each extent check deleted, the
header's checksum ignored, the delete confirmation gone, the destination builder
trusting its caller) and every one must fail it with a named verdict. A battery
that cannot fail is not a test.

One check is a lint rather than a lift: `err_t_lint()` reads the return types out of
`orb_sd.h` / `orb_theme.h` and fails any call site in `orb_theme.c` / `orb_net.c`
that puts `!` in front of a function declared `esp_err_t`. ESP_OK is 0, so
`if (!orb_sd_ensure_mounted())` means "if the card is mounted, refuse". It compiles
with no warning, and a bench with an empty socket cannot observe it. That inversion
shipped here first and is the mutation this lint owns.

Four real defects this suite caught before any card went in: a stray `'` in a
JS comment that broke the whole `/themes` `<script>`, `theme_file()` building its
own path with `snprintf` and validating nothing (it now delegates to
`orb_sd_theme_path()` so the extension append shares the validator and the
bounds check), a signed/unsigned comparison in the GIF-frame check
(`n_first < t->gif_frames + 1`, which silently promoted a `uint16_t` to a signed
int), and `orb_theme_upload_begin()` testing `orb_sd_ensure_mounted()` with `!`,
which inverted the mount test and refused every card that was present. glibc's
`strlcpy` differs from the IDF one, so the lifted code is renamed
`host_strlcpy`. That rename is the only edit the lift is allowed to make.

What hardware proved, and the two rules it cost a boot cycle each to learn, are in
the next section. Numbers measured on the board are there too.

## The card lock: two rules, and why the bench cannot see either

`orb_sd_lock()` is a plain non-recursive FreeRTOS mutex, and `esp_http_server`
services every session from ONE task at priority 4. A second take by the holder never
returns. The symptom is not a slow route: it is every web route stopping at once while
the dial keeps drawing and ping keeps replying, because the render task and lwip do not
need that mutex. A card lock bug therefore looks like a dead web server.

Three defects of that family were found on the board, none of them observable on the
bench:

1. `s_chunk` NULL on the upload path. The upload opens its `.part` with a bare `open()`
   and never passes through `card_open()`, so nothing had reserved the 4 KB buffer.
   FatFs stored through NULL and the dial died with a StoreProhibited at the end of a
   successful 3.87 MB upload. `ensure_chunk()` now sits at the top of
   `verify_container()`, `copy_into()` and `orb_theme_upload_begin()`: in
   `orb_theme_upload_begin()` it is an early no before the radio carries anything, and
   in the other two it is defensive and idempotent. `chunk_lint()` owns that rule.
2. Self-deadlock in `orb_theme_scan()`. It holds the lock across the whole directory
   walk and validated each entry through `card_open()`, which took the same mutex. An
   empty themes directory runs that loop body zero times, so the bench passed while the
   device wedged on the first real theme. `card_open()` is now a wrapper around
   `card_open_locked()`, and the scan calls the `_locked` pair.
3. The reverse mistake, made while fixing 2. `card_open_locked()` kept a
   `orb_sd_ensure_mounted()` call, which takes the mutex its caller already holds. The
   device's own new log line named it on the first boot: `waiting for the card: held by
   "httpd" for 16002 ms`.

Rule 1: nothing reachable between a take and the give that matches it may take the
mutex again. Call the `_locked` variant.

Rule 2: a lock holder must already be mounted. Mounting is the one card operation that
cannot happen while the mutex is held, because `orb_sd_ensure_mounted()` takes it.
`orb_theme_boot()` runs from `main.c:72` and `orb_sd_init()` only from `main.c:138`, so
the boot install can mount the card only through `card_open()`'s wrapper. With the check
in the core instead, a staged theme burned attempt 1 of 3 over a file the card had the
whole time, and the dial drew the baked-in bank with nothing to blame.

`lock_lint()` checks both rules: the held region is the first take to the LAST give
(not a depth walk, because `orb_theme_scan()` unlocks on an early return and a depth
walk then stops before the walk body), followed through the local call graph, plus the
cross-file lockers in `orb_sd.c`, plus a mount call before the first take. Both rules
are pinned by named mutations: `locked core re-asks for the mount it is holding` and
`card_open stops mounting before it locks`. `_code_only()` is not cosmetic: with doc
comments left in, `lock_lint()` reported three deadlocks that did not exist, from the
function names the comments discuss.

### Measured on the board (2026-10-10, SD16G SDHC 29.4 GB @ 20 MHz SPI)

| Step | Result |
|---|---|
| Upload 3 867 066 B | `state:stored` in 38.8 s and 48.1 s, RSSI -53 to -74 |
| `cfg.recv_wait_timeout` | 30 s. At the IDF default of 5 s a live 3.87 MB stream was aborted |
| Scan with one real theme | `listing /sd/themes` / `checking "kit3"` / `1 entry listed in 6 ms` |
| Scan with the directory emptied | `0 entries listed in 3 ms` |
| Load (full CRC of the container) | `stage "kit3": container verified in 2952 ms`, routes still answering |
| Boot install | `install of "kit3" from the card: ok`, `theme "kit3" from slot-B`, then `slot-A` |
| Clock | `art bank mapped: 3858874 B ... from slot-B`, bank at `@0x830000` not `@0x420000` |
| Delete the installed theme | `deleted "kit3" from the card (still installed on the dial)`, free_mb +3 |
| Delete, then reboot | boots straight into slot-B with no install attempt and no burned retry |
| Pending theme with the file gone | `theme install attempt 1 of 3 failed`, falls back to the baked-in bank |

Two method rules, both learned the hard way in this session:

- **Read the address from the serial log, not from memory.** The device prints
  `esp_netif_handlers: sta ip:` when DHCP completes. Two full rounds were lost probing a
  stale `192.168.68.251` after the board joined a different SSID at `192.168.2.32`. Every
  symptom of that (TCP connects, zero bytes, whole server "wedged") looked like the mutex
  bug and was not.
- **Capture serial unfiltered, then grep the file.** A keep-regex in the first capture
  script matched `orb_theme|orb_sd|load|card` and dropped the watchdog and boot lines, so
  a hang looked like silence. `orb_sd_lock()` now logs the holder task name, reports the
  wait every 5 s, and logs an ESP_LOGE on a same-task reentry; that instrumentation named
  the deadlock in one boot, which is the argument for putting it in before the bisecting.

## The on-dial menu, and the one knob gesture you cannot inject from the bench

`main/menu/menu_view.c` adds a third screen. `main/input/input_router.c` is still the
only reader of the knob; it now has two levels. Roaming, a turn swaps
MENU → HUE → CLOCK and a press arms the screen you are on. Armed, the screen owns the
turn and a long-press returns to the clock. Long-press no longer cycles screens —
one button cannot mean "next screen" two ways depending on how long it is held.

**Menu entry and exit (owner ruling, 2026-10-25).** Reaching the menu screen by any
route — a settled flick, a press on the chip, or a press while it shows — goes straight
into the menu list; the landing card never interposes. On a settled flick the router
arms the committed entry when its registry `kind` is `ORB_APP_KIND_SHELL` (a one-line
arm on the settle-commit, so the picker's two-level gate D25 is untouched). Exiting the
menu — the top row's exit and a long press at the top of the real menu, and a long press
from any menu sub-page with nothing open to collapse — returns to the entry that was on
the glass before the menu was entered, not to the landing card. The router records that
entry's **slug** on the switch into the shell (a card row can uninstall from inside the
menu, so a pointer would name a recycled slot), and `input_router_menu_exit()` resolves
it; an empty memory (boot landed on the menu) or a vanished row falls back to home.
`input_router_home()` still means "the landing" and is what the D38 share card parks
under — it is not the menu's exit door. The landing card survives only as the face of an
unarmed shell (a bench `?to=menu`, a share window parked over the dial). The long-press
collapse of an open option group (D12) and the app page's own first-refusal hook are
unchanged and take precedence.

**Menu page/cursor memory (item 3, same ruling).** The page and row the owner left
are persisted across an unarmed gap (exit, screen switch, reboot) and restored on the
next arm — one `orb_menu` NVS namespace, two i32 keys, written only on the disarm edge
idempotent against a RAM mirror (the `page_write`/`page_remember` idiom), so the steady
state costs zero commits. Only `MENU_POS_`-recorded pages restore: TOP, TZ, DISPLAY, TO
(the cursor is recomputed by `enter_to`), PERMS, ABOUT. LANDING never restores, and
PG_APP never does — its identity is a pointer to a card row that may be gone. On restore
a page's `enter()` hook wins over the stored row (the zone page marks the zone in force,
not where the cursor last was), and a stored row past a now-shorter live count is clamped
into it by `repaint()`'s pre-paint fit, so the first paint never shows an out-of-range
highlight. `host_menu_test.py` fakes `nvs_*` to run the shipped `pos_save`/`pos_load`
round-trip, and the four item-3 entries in its mutation list are the named ways this can
rot into a lie.

**The card calls must not run where the widgets are touched.** `orb_theme_scan()`
takes the card mutex for its whole walk and `orb_theme_stage_load()` measured 2952 ms;
either can additionally queue behind a browser upload holding that mutex for 38–48 s.
The router's poll is an `lv_timer` on the LVGL task and is simultaneously the dial's
animation and the only input reader, so both calls live on the `orb_menu` worker task
(4096 B, priority 3, matching the httpd worker that already runs them). `host_menu_test.py`
lints this: a blocking call in any function that also calls `lv_label_set_text()`/
`lv_obj_set_style_*()`/`lv_obj_invalidate()` fails the suite, and
`the card scan moves back onto the LVGL task` is one of its named mutations.

### Verifying a screen nobody turned a knob on

The encoder is three GPIOs driven only by a finger, so before this the menu and the
picker had never been entered on hardware. `GET /api/v1/screen` is the bench door:

| Query | Effect |
|---|---|
| `?to=<slug>` | switch to a registered app (posted, applied on the LVGL task, **unarmed** — the two-level menu stays driveable from the bench; a real settled flick onto the shell arms it, a request does not). Slugs come from the table in `main/app/orb_app.c`: `clock`, `color_picker` (alias `hue`), `menu`. An unknown slug is a 400 whose body lists the known ones — the value itself never reaches the response. |
| `?turn=N` | N synthetic detents, **electrical** sense: +1 = CW = up/right on screen |
| `?push=1` | one synthetic tap |
| `?hold=1` | the long-press escape gesture. From a non-menu screen it homes to the entry flagged `is_home` (C8). From an armed menu page with nothing to collapse it **exits the menu** to the entry shown before it (owner ruling 2026-10-25) — over HTTP there is no prior entry, so from `?to=menu` it falls back to home. |

**Read the cursor before you trust a turn.** Until 2026-10-11 the menu said nothing about
where its highlight was — `repaint()` moved the highlight and that was the only answer — so
three scripted walks drove it blind and every one of them believed it had arrived somewhere
else. Two pressed the `IT_AP` stub three times thinking they were choosing DISPLAY; a third
fired `JOB_TZ` twice and left the dial on Europe/London while believing it had committed the
dim timeout. `menu_view_apply_input()` now logs `cursor <PAGE> row N/M: <label>` on every
move, and a walk that ignores that line is not to be trusted. `page_hint()`/`page_title()`
already carried the page name; the log was the half that was missing.

The clamp at row 0 is the specific thing the echo distinguishes: `if (next == s_cur) return;`
means a turn already at the top of the list produces **no event at all**, which from the LAN
is indistinguishable from a detent the decoder swallowed — the exact symptom the owner
reports as "sometimes the notch does nothing". Note also that the injected sign is the
electrical one: `?turn=-1` walks the cursor DOWN from row 0, because the delta arrives at the
router already signed and does not pass through the encoder's own sign handling.

Inject the long press too: it is the only exit from an armed screen, so a broken
one soft-locks the dial with rotate belonging to the list. Prove it — verified here
as armed zone-list → `menu idle` → `screen -> clock` inside one 10 ms poll.

Two bench traps, both hit for real on this feature:

- **Reset before a scripted walk.** A previous run can leave `s_inj_delta` set if it
  exited before the 10 ms poll drained it, and the next run then swerves to another
  screen ~500 ms in with no request behind it. Real detents cannot go stale, so this
  is harness hygiene, not a firmware defect.
- **Wait for the radio, do not sleep.** After a reset the association ladder costs
  ~5 s at −57 dBm; poll `/api/v1/status` until it answers or the first request of the
  walk times out and reads as a crash.
- **A stalled HTTP body at rssi near −55 is the bench RF, not the firmware.** The
  symptom: status line and headers arrive, body bytes never do; small bodies
  (<1 KB) still complete. Hit for real on 2026-10-12 with the /apps listing: the
  same hammer run failed `/themes` on a known-good image as badly as on the new
  one (ok=2/20 control vs ok=0/20 suspect, rssi −55 both). Bisect the antenna
  before bisecting the commit: run the identical walk against a previous image
  and compare rates.

It reaches only `input_router_request()`/`input_router_inject()`, cannot touch the
card, radio or bootloader, and sits beside `/api/v1/reboot` and `/api/v1/ota`, which
are far more capable. `input_router.c` is still the only code that calls
`lv_screen_load()`.

### The trap that cost two wasted hardware runs

**One detent per event is a clamp, not a bug.** `menu_view_apply_input()` moves the
cursor a single row whatever the magnitude of `delta`, so a burst of `+2` in one poll
is ONE row of travel — deliberate, because a fast turn must not fly past the row you
were heading for. Both my first drivers asked for `turn=2` expecting two rows, landed
on TZ instead of THEME, and I read that as a routing bug twice. Drive it with one
`?turn=1` request per row, and read the row you are on from the server's own
`/api/v1/sd` `themes` list rather than from an assumed order.

Also: the card must actually hold a *non-active* theme. Staging the theme already on
the dial is correctly refused with "is already on the dial" and leaves `pending` empty,
which looks identical to a broken staging path. Upload a second container under a
different name to test the real transition.

`/api/v1/status` does **not** report a staged theme. Read `pending` from `/api/v1/sd`.

## How the knob is measured, and what decides how it feels

`main/knob/knob.c`. `knob_rot_isr` shares one `s_prevAB` across A and B, decodes each
edge through `kQuadTable`, and calls `knob_edge()` — the one function that decides
whether an edge was a click. `knob_edge()`, `knob_detent_from_net()` and
`knob_detents_rounded()` are pure over plain integers and take time as an argument, so
`tools/host_knob_test.py` lifts them verbatim and replays synthetic AB state sequences.
That is the only way any of this was getable right: the three reported failures below
each survived being read twice.

**A detent is an arrival at the valley, not a count of edges.** The valley is the
quadrature state the detent spring parks the shaft in — measured on this unit as
`knob: valley 3 (11)` at boot, i.e. A=B=HIGH. On arrival at it, the net travel since
the last arrival decides the direction, and one arrival credits at most ONE detent.
`knob_poll()` then settles anything left over once the stream has been idle for
`KNOB_SETTLE_MS`, which recovers clicks whose valley edge never arrived.

Why the edge count had to go. The reference driver (and my first two attempts here)
committed after a full detent of net raw travel, and the owner's report after that
design was three complaints in one sentence — **"it moves mostly one but sometimes two
and sometimes none"**:

| Report | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| first notch does nothing | the click's fourth edge lands past the click stop, so a hand stopping on the notch never reaches a whole-detent gate | net travel, not edge count; the settle recovers the short click |
| sometimes two | make-bounce lands extra net steps in ONE notch, and an edge counter cannot tell that from two clicks | one credit per valley arrival, plus `KNOB_CLICK_GUARD_MS` (20 ms — above any contact bounce, below any wrist) |
| sometimes none | edges lost between pin and ISR leave no valley arrival | the idle settle rounds the stranded net into detents |

Rocking a knob is still inert, which is the hysteresis the reference needed and the
reason a naive commit-on-every-edge is not the answer: out-and-back nets zero, so
nothing is credited. `host_knob_test.py` asserts all three failure modes and catches
16/16 mutations, including "restore the edge-count rule", so the regression cannot come
back quietly.

**`KNOB_STEPS_PER_DETENT` is a property of the unit, not a feel knob.** Read
`knob.spd10` on `/api/v1/status` after turning it by hand: 40 means four edges per
click as assumed, 20 means the constant is wrong by half — indistinguishable from the
hand. `knob.vhits` against `knob.shits` splits the detents by how they arrived; a
session that is mostly `shits` means edges are being lost between the pin and the ISR,
which is wiring or interrupt latency and no retuning of the rule will fix.

### The turn direction, and where it is allowed to be interpreted

The encoder sits on the case side above the dial's centre, so the arc the finger
travels is not the arc the list scrolls. `KNOB_DIR_DOWN_IS_CW` (0 now) is the single
switch. What a committed turn *means* comes only from `knob_row_step()` and
`knob_col_step()` in `knob.c`; `knob_take_delta()` keeps the raw electrical sense
(CW = positive) so the bench door and any future gesture code see the truth.
**A screen that negates a delta itself is a bug** — `host_knob_test.py` fails on
`(delta > 0 ? 1 : -1)` appearing in a screen file.

The owner's ruling, on record because the mounting fixes it and the code cannot
derive it:

> the direction that means **DOWN** is the good direction for **LEFT**;
> the direction that means **UP** is the good direction for **RIGHT**.

`knob_col_step()` is `knob_row_step()` negated, so a future horizontal screen calls it
and cannot drift out of that agreement, and flipping the one constant moves both axes
together. Today: CW = up = right, CCW = down = left. The hue editor deliberately reads
the raw delta instead — a value is not a list position — with the reason recorded at
its call site.

Verified on hardware 2026-10-10 (`/tmp/dirwalk2.py`): baseline `US/Mountain`, one
injected `?turn=-1` then a press applied `US/Pacific` (one row down) and one
`?turn=+1` applied `US/Central` (one row up).

**Bench trap, hit for real:** `?turn=N` is the *electrical* sense and the screens now
map it, so any saved walk script that used `?turn=1` to step **down** a list now steps
**up** and silently selects the wrong row — `/tmp/homewalk.py` still passes its
escape-hatch checks while pressing `AP (stub)` instead of `TZ`. Read the row you reached
out of the serial log (`menu: AP selected`), not out of the script's intent.

**Injection proves nothing about the encoder.** `/api/v1/screen?turn=N` enters at
`input_router_inject()` and never touches `knob_rot_isr`, so `knob.raw`/`knob.det` stay
at zero through a fully successful injected walk. Any claim about quadrature, the
valley, the commit rule or debounce needs a finger on the knob and a read of
`/api/v1/status`'s `knob` block (`tools/turnsession.py` drives exactly that).

### Measured on the board (2026-10-10, menu build)

| Step | Result |
|---|---|
| Boot | `menu ready (worker up, 62 zones, no card)`, `router running (poll 10 ms, 3 screens)`, boots on the clock, no new crash |
| Theme scan off-task | `listing /sd/themes` → `1 entry listed in 5 ms`, dial kept 6 fps throughout |
| Worker stack | `job N done, 608 B stack never used` (scan) / `336 B` — headroom, not exhaustion |
| TZ apply through the menu | `US/Central` → `US/Eastern`, status offset `-300` → `-240`, then restored to `US/Central` via the same path |
| Theme stage through the menu | `stage "kit4": container verified in 3900 ms` → `staged "kit4"` → `job 1 done`, poll still answering |
| Decline the staged theme | `orb_theme_unstage()` from the menu's own row; `pending` back to `""` |
| HSB two-level gate | unarmed turn logged `screen -> clock` and no colour change; armed turns logged `-> H = 211/212/213`, push logged `push H -> S` |
| Cost | app 0x15e590 → 0x15ff30 (~7 kB) |

Two refusals that are features, not bugs — both cost me a debugging cycle here:

- Staging the theme already on the dial answers `"x" is already on the dial` and leaves
  `pending` empty, which looks exactly like a broken staging path. Test with a second,
  non-active container.
- `orb_theme_stage_load()` rejects a container whose header slug disagrees with its
  filename: `the file calls itself "kit3" — rename one of them`. Copying a `.orbtheme`
  to a second name therefore does not create a second theme; patch `W_SLUG`/`W_TITLE`
  and re-run `_hdr_crc()`, leaving the pixel payload byte-identical.

Since P5.2 (`924d9ec`) one `const menu_page_t s_pages[PG_N]` table is the whole page
model, so `tools/host_menu_test.py` reads the table instead of a set of switches. It
lifts the model block, the table, every hook it names, and `activate()` — the press
paths run on the host now, because the dispatcher no longer touches LVGL. It checks
179 things: the clamp at both ends, the no-wrap rule, the 62-row zone list with its
back row, each sub-page's back landing, the D19 cap rows and the worker's pack, the
press of every page, a worker result landing while the screen is disarmed (which must
not paint a list on the title card), and since P5.3 the APPS catalog's four states,
its two worker doors and the two-press uninstall — including the detent that cancels
the arm. Since P5.4 the painted header carries the position readout on any page
long enough to scroll — `title_line()` is the one composer, gated on the window's
own `rows > ROW_MAX` rather than on a per-page flag, and the checks pin its exact
text at the boundary in both directions (5 rows: no digits; 6: `1/6`). The walk
driver still reads the bare title in the cursor log line on purpose: that line's
`row N/M` is the same `s_cur`/`page_rows()` pair the header composes, so the 229
records the walk checks are the numbers the readout prints. The digits themselves
are host-pinned — the bench has no framebuffer readback door. The top page's row
indices are derived from `top_os_rows()` rather than
written out, so a legitimate new OS row does not redden a row-order check. The
damaged-theme row is no longer this file's subject: B2 gave the theme list to the
clock app.

`--mutations` must stay red on all seventy-three. Six of the checks are structural
lints that read the table's text and pin the switch model dead, so a second way to
say what a page IS fails the suite even when every behavioural check passes. One
round of the battery "survived" eleven mutants because the lifts were taken at
import and the mutated text never reached the compiler: if you touch this suite,
check it can still fail.

The lifted labels, titles and hints are the shipped strings, so identical rendered
text is pinned here rather than on the glass. The frame-diff walk ran 2026-10-13:
`e74d500` (pre-table) against `185af12` (P5.2), 221 cursor records each, 49 checks
0 failed both sides, and the only diff line is the boot's own image stamp. The
driver is `tools/bench_menu_walk.py` (closed-loop, nudge-based — `go_to()` and
back emit no line, so every landing is read by injecting one detent); re-run it
before signing off any change to a `rows()`/`label()`/`back_cur`. If the bench
radio will not carry `/api/v1/screen`, say the frame-diff line is pending and
point at this suite — do not claim a rendered-output pass.

  the deadlock in one boot, which is the argument for putting it in before the bisecting.

## Testing the Watch Composer: deploy it to Windows and drive Chrome there

The composer (`tools/watch-composer/Watch Composer.dc.html`) is a browser app. Its real
behaviour lives in canvas pixels, file dialogs, and a localStorage asset store, so those
three must be exercised in the Windows Chrome. **This WSL box reaches that Chrome directly:
the host binary is `/mnt/c/Program Files/Google/Chrome/Application/chrome.exe`, and it runs
`--headless=new --dump-dom` from here.** No need to ask the user to open a browser — drive
it headless. A bare `chrome.exe --version` attaches to the running GUI session and prints
nothing useful; always pass `--headless=new`. The GUI instance is usually open, so a headless
run needs its own `--user-data-dir` (a fresh temp dir under `C:\Users\greg\AppData\Local\Temp`)
or it silently reuses the live profile and dumps the wrong page.

Ask for "test the composer" and the expected action is these two commands:

```sh
cd /home/greg/docker/Projects/gorb-os
python3 tools/watch-composer/deploy_test_folder.py            # -> /mnt/c/Users/greg/Downloads/test
~/.local/share/nodeenv-gorb/bin/node tools/watch-composer/_selftest/headless-test.js   # the WSL-side node gate

# the browser suites, through the host Chrome. Launch detached: a headless run of
# export-test/bake3/motion exceeds the 30 s inline tool cap (it boots React from unpkg
# first). Redirect to a log and poll it, per the rule at the top of this file.
CHROME='/mnt/c/Program Files/Google/Chrome/Application/chrome.exe'
cd /mnt/c/Users/greg/Downloads/test   # the deployed copy; file:// must resolve to Windows paths
"$CHROME" --headless=new --disable-gpu --no-sandbox \
  --user-data-dir='C:\Users\greg\AppData\Local\Temp\wc_headless' \
  --virtual-time-budget=120000 \
  --dump-dom 'file:///C:/Users/greg/Downloads/test/_selftest/export-test.html' \
  > /tmp/wc_export_dump.html 2>/tmp/wc_chrome.log
```

Every Chrome output line ends in `\r` (drvfs); pipe through `tr -d '\r'` before grepping. The
verdict is the dumped `<title>` and the last `RESULT:` line, not the exit code — Chrome exits 0
on a page that printed FAIL. The suites need internet: `support.js` boots React, ReactDOM and
Babel from unpkg and the `<helmet>` pulls ~40 families from Google Fonts; offline, the page
renders blank and reads as broken when it is not.

The deploy script copies the four files the app loads (`Watch Composer.dc.html`,
`support.js`, `dial-renderer.js`, `watch-engine.js`) into the target root, and all of
`_selftest/` into a `_selftest/` subfolder. That depth is load-bearing: the selftest
pages load `../watch-engine.js`. Flatten the layout and every probe 404s its engine.
Each copy is compared by sha256 before and after, because a drvfs cache can hand Chrome
stale JS with no error at all.

Run each browser suite the same way, swapping the file name: `export-test.html` (end to
end, the verdict rules below), `bake3-test.html` (FORMAT 3 in real pixels, greps for a
`B64:` line to hand `tools/verify_js_bake.py`), and `motion-test.html`. The app itself at
`file:///C:/Users/greg/Downloads/test/Watch%20Composer.dc.html` is only worth opening by
hand for the human-confirm checks far below — a headless `--dump-dom` cannot see the canvas.

Verdict rules, so a skipped block cannot read as success:

- `export-test.html`: the tab title must read `PASS` and the last report line must be
  `RESULT: PASS`. The custom-art block prints its own line starting `custom art: backdrop
  in the plate pixels`. Missing that line means the block never ran. The five evidence
  lines, and `?ONLY=feat` to run the pixel block alone when the suite stops printing
  without a verdict, are listed by `deploy_test_folder.py`.
- Headless suites: each must print a nonzero check count. A suite reporting green with a
  suspiciously low count stopped exercising something.
- After editing the component class, regenerate `_selftest/component.js` (the recipe is in
  `_selftest/README.md`). It is an extracted copy, and a stale one keeps the UI suites green
  while they test last week's code.

What only a human can confirm in the app, after uploading art in the Dial and Hands tabs:

- the plate backdrop paints under an openwork dial and the apertures, and clips to the dial
  circle under a drawn case — that is "Background art · behind the geartrain". Under Case
  "None" the same art runs to the panel corners instead, because its own pixels are the case
- the dial-face art reads as a printed dial: chapter print, numerals and applied markers stay
  legible ON TOP of it, and openwork and the apertures cut it away — "Dial surface art"
- the two slots are independent: different images, different offsets, and one image may fill
  both without the store counting it twice
- the fit gate refuses an upscale, and names both sizes in the refusal
- Zoom rescales the art about its centre without moving it off its offset, and the alignment
  guide's frame tracks it exactly — the box drawn on the stage is the box that was painted
- clicking the dial or a hand selects it AND opens the pane that can change it; clicking the
  same pixel again steps down through whatever overlaps there, so nothing under the top layer
  is unreachable
- and the pane then SCROLLS to that item's own row (a hand's pivot fields, a complication's
  row, the art section of the slot being panned) — switching tabs without moving the view is
  the half-fix that reads as working until the field you wanted is below the fold
- Escape, or the Deselect button under the stage, clears the selection outright: with the dial
  selected every pixel of the face is the dial, so no click can deselect it; the Guide button
  beside it pins the overlay on or off independently of the selection
- dragging the dial pans the art slot the chip names, not the other one
- dragging a hand moves its arbour, and while the drag is held the hand stops sweeping so the
  geometry under the cursor cannot rotate away
- the virtual hand reads: dashed sprite, arbour cross, sweep ring and the intended-direction
  ray. Set Facing to bring the ink onto the ray — art drawn pointing anywhere but 12 is fine
  as long as Facing says where it points
- a hand-sprite pivot outside the visible ink is accepted and reported as such, and the
  thumbnail frames the ink inside the grown plane
- "None" (no case) draws NOTHING at all: no metal, no rim shade, no mask, and no clip on the
  author's own art, which is what carries the device's case and must reach the panel corners.
  The composited pixels are identical with that layer shown or hidden, and identical at any
  opening — the slider only says where the hardware begins, so the guide can draw the ring
- the alignment guide's reference (hub cross, 24 spokes from it, range rings, comp
  centres) is pinned to the watch and does not move while the dial art is dragged; the
  art's own frame and centre are drawn in blue precisely so the two are not confused
- a window set to a round aperture is visibly round, including its frame and its digit
- the hand-sprite pivot marker drags, and hub-snap lands on the visible ink centroid
- a transparent sub-dial drops its plate, keeps its hosted window on the bare dial, and
  its pointer keeps a real pivot instead of a hub cap
- Export ships the layer kit plus the `.wckit`, and reloading keeps every upload

The asset store is one shared `file://` origin in Chrome. Every local copy of the
composer — repo folder, Downloads copy — reads and writes the same `wc.assets` key, so
clearing it in one clears it everywhere. That sharing is also what makes redeploying safe:
uploaded art survives the copy.

## Orb Manager landing page: one orb-ness verdict, two doors (2026-10-14)

The fleet list and the New Orb button are complements, and they stop being
complements the moment each side derives "is this an orb?" on its own. The first
version did: the list hid a row with no name/ip/fw/state, and the button hid any
port whose MAC matched a fleet row (`inFleet`). A cable sighting alone writes that
row — `Store.Confirm` runs on every attributed sweep — so a wiped board on a lead
rendered no card and no New Orb button either. It vanished from the page while
`/api/ports` listed it happily, which is what "two orbs plugged in, one shows up"
looked like from the operator's side.

The rule now lives server-side, stated once:

- `orbRow.provesOrb` (discover.go) decides the row: a name, an address, a firmware
  stamp, a reported state, a provisioning AP, a liveness verdict, a LAN sighting, or
  an Identity of `confirmed`/`claimed`. `descriptor` — the host's USB serial number —
  is deliberately NOT proof.
- `storeProvesOrb` (fleet.go) is the same rule over the address book's fields, and it
  is what `handlePorts` writes as `orbSeen` on each port entry, plus the `espressif`
  and `inFleet` fields it already sent.
- Both fields are non-omitempty on both endpoints, so an absent verdict reads as a no
  and the page fails closed. The client reads `o.orb` (`isBlankRow`) and
  `e.orbSeen` (`isBlankPort`) and never re-derives either.
- A wipe calls `clearChipRead`, because the chip's word described a firmware that no
  longer exists. Leaving it would keep a blank board `confirmed` and therefore
  invisible to the one door that can refill it.

Pinned by `TestCableOnlyBoardIsNotAnOrbAndStaysAdoptable` and
`TestStoreProvesOrbNeedsMoreThanARow` (roster_test.go) and
`TestClientReadsTheOrbVerdictAndNothingElse` (jscheck_test.go, which greps app.js for
the banned field-by-field rules). Before changing either gate, re-read the comment on
`orbRow.Orb`: the bug is silent on the wire and total on screen.

Also on the bench: an Add-by-IP test against a real hub writes the operator's state
file (`~/.config/orb-manager/state.json`), because a second hub shares it. Run test
hubs with `-addr` on a scratch HOME, or clean the file after; a grant for 127.0.0.1
there is a test artifact, not an orb.

## Four bench-and-tool gotchas that cost time on 2026-10-15

- **A killed streaming flash CANCELS the write.** The 30 s tool cap does not merely
  abandon the `curl`: the hub sees the client context close and stops the session at its
  next block boundary, so the board is left in its bootloader at 96 %. A verification
  flash must go through a script file launched with the `setsid … & disown` idiom above,
  with the stream redirected to a file you poll for the `"done"` event.
- **`pkill -f <pattern>` matches the killing shell's own command line** and kills it, so
  the restart you asked for never happens and the tool call returns SIGTERM. Kill by the
  PID from `ss -ltnp`, or take `pgrep -n orb-manager` into a variable first.
- **Each command in one tool call is its own shell starting at the workspace root.** A
  `cd` in an earlier element does not carry to a later one, so a relative path in element
  two reads a different file than element one wrote, or fails outright. Give every element
  its own `cd` or an absolute path. Related trap: `~/docker/Projects/gorb-os-2` is a
  **different clone on a different branch**, and a root-relative recursive grep sweeps
  both and shows functions that do not exist in the tree you are editing.
- **Build `firmware` BEFORE `manager`, then restart the hub.** The bundle is embedded at
  compile time (`//go:embed`), so a rebuild order that compiles first and refreshes the
  zip second leaves a running hub serving the previous image — and a flash driven by that
  hub "verifies" a fix that is not in it. Check `grep 'orb-manager v' <log>` against the
  binary's mtime when a firmware change appears not to take effect.
- **The hub listens on `127.0.0.1:3779`, and there is no `/api/ports/release`.** The port
  is printed at boot as `orb-manager vX on http://127.0.0.1:3779`; read the log rather
  than assuming 3000, and drive port claims through the operations registry, not a route
  invented from memory.
- **A hub that claims a USB serial port resets the board.** `serialprov.go` enters
  download mode on claim, so the device log shows `rst:0x15, boot:0x23` and any in-flight
  HTTP dies mid-body. That reset reads exactly like a firmware crash. Detach the cable
  from WSL before bench-testing a board over HTTP, and stop blaming firmware until
  `rst_source` on a clean boot says otherwise.
- **A fleet liveness sweep cuts a theme plane stream short.** Both want the device's one
  httpd task, and the sweep wins. The hub therefore holds the sweep off while a plane
  relay runs and retries once on `orbnet.ErrTornPreview`; do not remove either when
  touching `fanoutLiveness` or `handleThemePreview`.

## Flashing lives behind one button, and the gate that took an explicit yes was the wrong shape (2026-10-19)

The merged-`.bin` feature landed as a project gate plus a refusal that opened into a
paragraph. The operator's verdict on the result: wordy, and no visible way out. Both
halves were the same mistake, and the lesson is broader than the feature.

- **A refusal rendered as prose is a dead end with extra steps.** The card showed the
  hub's sentence plus a "Flash it anyway" button nobody located. Put a decision in a
  modal where the choices are the whole surface, and keep prose to the one line that
  states what the chosen act costs.
- **`ESP32-S3-Touch-AMOLED-1.75-FactoryOnly-260805.bin` was refused correctly and
  uselessly.** The hub read `esp32-s3-touch-amoled-brookesia` from its descriptor —
  DFRobot's factory demo for this exact panel, a valid S3 whole-flash image — and said
  the hub only flashes gorb-os releases. True, final, useless. The gate existed to stop a
  silent network push of a stray build to 0x0. Over a cable the operator picked the file
  up and plugged it in, so a second question there answers nothing. The gate is now
  positional rather than conversational: a file from disk is reachable only on the cable
  branch of the flash modal, which sends `allow_foreign=1` itself. CLI parity is
  `--allow-foreign`. The flag is not gone — it still gates every scripted caller, which
  has no cable to imply intent.
- **`probe=1` is what let the warning go away.** `POST /api/firmware` validates and
  reports without adopting when the field is set, so the modal names the project on the
  file row before the operator commits. A label reading
  `esp32-s3-touch-amoled-brookesia` beats a paragraph about foreign firmware: same fact,
  positioned where a wrong file gets spotted.
- **Version comparisons do not belong on the cable path.** `planFor` ignores the mode
  argument and writes the full manifest: bootloader, partition table, otadata, both app
  slots. Correct from any version in any direction, so a breaking-change detector there
  would be a gate with nothing behind it. The question is real only for the spare-slot
  OTA. Its answer is the hardware: `CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE=y` reverts an
  image that never marks itself valid, so a downgrade over Wi-Fi is offered behind one
  confirm (`overridable: true`, `verdict: "downgrade"`, retry with `force: true`) rather
  than refused. Reading the on-device layout instead is unavailable: the ROM answers
  `MEM_READ` and `SPI_READ` with the `[1,5]` no-stub failure, which is also why
  **Read what this board runs** (was *Identify this orb*, a label that said nothing) must
  reboot the board to read the bootloader's console line. Keep the reboot cost visible:
  it is the reason the control is a press and not a refresh.
- **Gates that decide survivability stay shut on every path:** the 16 MB ceiling and
  0x9000 floor, the `0xE9` header, the chip ID, the zip-bomb caps, descriptor hygiene. A
  blob naming no project is refused with no second press, cable included — a confirmation
  with no name to quote back asks for trust rather than attention. The partition-table
  shape gate is the one dropped under the flag, and only there: a vendor's layout is not
  ours to grade, the write is verbatim, and `esprom.NvsRegions` already fails the wipe at
  the door that needs a table.
- **"Reset" named two acts and the card offered only the destructive one.** Erasing the
  whole flash to change a Wi-Fi password is why the label was wrong. **Reset this orb**
  opens a modal with **Settings** (`/api/orb/cmd` `RESET ALL`: firmware stays) and
  **Firmware** (`/api/lonely`: base image, board stops being an orb). Checking Firmware
  forces Settings, because the base image re-provisions anyway — two boxes, not four
  states. No typed word anywhere; the hub's `LONELY` payload gate stays for every
  non-browser caller. The `lonely` name is an inside joke and stays out of
  operator-facing strings.
- **The post-flash panel had no exit anyone could see.** `joinDone` filled `#ob-done` with
  **Open this orb** (navigates away) and **Change network** (reopens the form). Both move
  the operator somewhere. Backdrop and Escape did close it, and nothing said so. A
  completed action needs a control that says it is completed: `#ob-close`, **Done**,
  closes the dialog and stays on the hub.

The UI's six firmware rows are now one button and one modal. `ui_test.go` pins the shape.

`web/app.js` is one `<script>` with no build step, so a syntax error anywhere discards the
whole file: every handler stays unwired and the page sits on its loading placeholders with
nothing in the hub's log to point at the line. Two tests hold that boundary, because Go
cannot parse JavaScript and there is no engine on this box:

  `TestAppJSAssignsOnlyDeclaredNames`, plus its own negative case. Rewriting the modal
  deleted a `let buildVersion` line and left the assignment behind, which becomes a silent
  global in a browser.
  `TestAppJSProseStaysInComments`, plus its own negative case. A splice lost the `//` on one
  comment continuation line at column zero. Braces stayed balanced and quotes stayed paired,
  so every balance test passed, and the hub served a dead page.
  `TestAppJSWritesResolveInScope`, plus its own negative case. A later revert deleted
  `const btn = $("#mn-flashbtn")` from `gateFwButtons` and left `btn.disabled` behind, so
  every fleet tile click threw a ReferenceError inside `openOrbByIP` before `show("orb")`:
  tiles looked selectable and did nothing. A name declared in *some* function is not
  declared in *this* one, so this check walks lexical scopes: `function` bodies, arrow
  bodies at any depth, and top-level `for`/`if`/`try` blocks all bind their own names, and
  only text outside every region counts as the file's top level.

Check parseability with esprima before trusting a green suite
(`python3 -c "import esprima; esprima.parseScript(open('web/app.js').read())"`), and run
the real client against a stub DOM under the node binary this box carries at
`~/.local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/nodejs_wheel/bin/node` (it has no npm, so no jsdom:
a hand-written element stub is enough to catch a boot throw and a broken click path).
Re-fetch the **served** file after `make manager`: assets are embedded, so a running hub
serves the bytes it was built with, never the ones on disk.
## Store-path stack overflow and the httpd boot squeeze — measured 2026-10-24, orb-B

- `orb_jobs`'s 4096 B worker **no longer fits the theme-store chain**: two
  stores of the 4.94 MB tourbillon overflowed it identically (PC 0x4037c87b,
  during `copy_bundle3`), each after the erase, so each crash left the single
  slot torn and the dial on the base face. The fix in the tree raises the
  worker to 8192 with the watermark line as proof; read `orb_jobs.c`'s header
  comment before touching either number.
- A torn slot is not a neutral state: same-day boots with the torn slot
  measured `pre-netif internal free=65215` against `78311` on a whole-slot
  boot — ~13 KB gone before `orb_net` runs (`app: menu: faulting HOME entry`
  is present on both sides and did not explain it; unnamed). Under that
  penalty **httpd's boot-instant task create fails** (`ESP_ERR_HTTPD_TASK`,
  `post-wifi_start largest=7168/8192/12288` all died; the P5.5 ladder's slack
  is gone on HEAD) — page dead, store unreachable, repair impossible. This
  cascades from the overflow: crash → torn slot → starved boot → dead page.
- A failed `httpd_start` **leaks its listen socket**: IDF frees the server
  (`esp_http_server/src/httpd_main.c:469`) but closes fds only on the normal
  stop path (`:350`). Every later attempt in the same boot then dies at
  `httpd_server_init: error in listen (112)` — the ten-second deferred-retry
  loop observed all evening never recovers. Deferring the start after a
  failure is pointless: fix the boot instant or reboot.
- The stale-`PROJECT_VER` trap bit the reasoning, not just the stamp: the
  evening's only httpd-healthy boot ran the session-start binary (stamped
  `14884ae`, compile-time 16:46:36 — the audit log names it), so "the 8192
  build booted fine" was false. When a boot behaves strangely, `grep
  "Compile time\|App version"` the capture BEFORE comparing heap ladders.
- The 1739 ms Skeleton-store success line predates the current HEAD on this
  board; `orb_jobs_post(bundle_job)` at 4096 has since failed for BOTH
  bundle sizes tested (2.6 MB and 4.9 MB). Bench rule: after any store-path
  crash, land a good store in the same session before the pool penalty
  compounds.
## The reclaim boot — measured 2026-10-24 late, same board

- With `orb_jobs` at 8192 AND the two orb_net statics reclaimed (`ota_buf`
  4,096 + `s_scan_json` 1,408 to lazy PSRAM), the board **boots stable**: one
  boot, WiFi creds accepted and stored, sntp synced, mDNS announcing, no
  reboot for the rest of the capture. The crash-loop half of the cascade is
  dead. The repair itself needed no full factory reflash — the regenerated
  `storage` image at /tmp (ledger cleared, creds re-injected) held across
  boots, and the torn slot self-healed on the first good boot.
- **httpd is fixed as of the 23:01 build.** The +9 KB hunt ended with one
  symbol: `s_lay_key` in `clock_view.c` (2,960 B incl. `s_lay_handle`) was the
  entire regression versus the last known-good map — `mapdiff` of
  `gorb-os-2/build/gorb-os.map` against the live one showed it as the only
  `.bss` symbol grown ≥ 256 B. It moved to lazy PSRAM beside its siblings
  (`s_wp_x0/x1` 1,864 B in the same file, `s_chk_*` ~3.5 KB in `orb_theme.c`
  — the latter had a second, unreferenced duplicate set at line ~1194, also
  removed). The real bar measured by the new retry ladder in `orb_net.c`:
  `httpd_create` + `httpd_server_init` consume ~5.6 KB of the hole before the
  single task spawn (IDF 5.4 has ONE xTaskCreate, no separate ctrl task — the
  older "12 KB" figure in this file was right, the two-task story was wrong).
  Post-fix boot: `post-wifi_start largest=17,408`, `httpd_start` succeeds on
  the 8,192 tier first try, `post-httpd_start internal free=7,663`. The
  ladder (8192→6144→4096 with heap state per miss) stays as the instrument;
  a TASK-failure retry is futile — the first attempt's socketpair lands in
  TIME_WAIT and retries die `error in listen (112)` — so the ladder's value
  is the print, not the retry.
- **USB disk mode failed `lun: ESP_ERR_NO_MEM` for the same heap reason, in
  a different door** (owner hit it 23:07): the component's storage object
  embeds the 8 KB write buffer inline (`MSC_BUFSIZE=8192`) and allocs it as
  ONE `MALLOC_CAP_DMA` calloc; `orb_sd` lends the display tile ring only
  inside `orb_sd_lock()/unlock()` pairs, so by the time
  `tinyusb_msc_new_storage_sdmmc()` asked, the ring was funded back and the
  largest DMA block was 7,168. Fixed by holding `display_dma_borrow()` across
  driver-install + storage-new + tinyusb-install in `orb_usb_msc_start`
  (measured window: largest 19,456). Held through the whole share window on
  purpose: flushes drop, the panel holds its GRAM (frozen dial), and stop is
  a reboot anyway. Bench-trap: the glass freezes the moment share arms — by
  design, not a crash. Not yet bench-pressed: the only door is the menu row
  "Restart to share disk", so the owner's next press is the live test.
- **Preview is a hub page, never a firmware page (D28/D33):** the theme
  editor/preview is orb-manager's Watch Composer at
  `http://127.0.0.1:3779/composer/` (`web/composer/dial-renderer.js`).
  `orb_net.c` contains zero previews by design. SD theme load is
  firmware-side and live at `http://<orb>/themes` — it lists the CARD, so an
  empty card reads as "No themes on the card yet. Upload a .orbtheme below"
  and the page's own Upload button writes to the card. If the page is gone
  AND the composer is gone, check two things: `httpd` (boot heap lines) and
  whether something killed `bin/orb-manager`.
- **Boot-loop reports from a wired board with the hub running are the HUB,
  until proven otherwise — and the proven fix is in, 2026-10-24 23:30.**
  Three self-sustaining loops, all reading one board's reboot-blink as a
  brand-new cable: (1) `cableProof.retain` deleted a port's throttle clock
  the moment the port left the sweep, so the five-second sweep pinged the
  "new" board forever — fixed with `cableProofGoneGrace` (45 s; a same-MAC
  return keeps its clock, a MAC swap drops it); (2) on this board's
  USB-Serial-JTAG console, a plain host open asserts DTR/RTS and the ROM's
  auto-reset can land the chip in the download stub — `cableConsolePing` now
  always leaves with `esprom.HardReset` (the identity.go pattern): the board
  is handed back running its app, never stranded; (3) the paid probe's own
  reboot made the liveness dial fail 10 s later, `noteWiredSilence` flagged
  it, and the flag bought a fresh reset per tick — fixed by arming
  `expectSelfReboot` around the paid pulse and re-arming it when the probe
  ENDS (the handshake eats ten seconds off the front of the 90 s window), so
  the silence a probe caused excuses itself until the dial answers. The old
  plan claim that the PING "forces no reboot" is false for USB-JTAG leads:
  on this bench the door costs at most one boot per window, and the throttle
  is what bounds it now. `-probe-cables=false` only skips the launch burst;
  the sweep doors run under every flag and are the ones that were pulsing.
  `selfRebootExcused` keys on `macBare` — colon-vs-bare MAC forms have
  already fooled this guard once.
- **The sweep asks the LAN before it pulses (2026-10-26).** The identity cache is
  memory, so a hub restart left every orb on a lead with no answer on file and costed
  each one a reboot at launch. `launchprobe.go`'s `lanProof` now runs between the gate
  owing a question and `probeCableNow` pulsing it: one `GET /api/v1/status` at the
  address the fleet row holds for the descriptor's MAC, filed as `provenLan` when the
  reply names that same MAC. Four cases fall through and still pay the probe: no row,
  an address on `192.168.4.x`, a silent door, a door naming another chip. `provenLan`
  is never a contradiction (`contradicts` refuses it), survives a `doneFailed` probe,
  ages on the ordinary TTL, and is renewed free by the ten-second liveness dial — so an
  orb the network can see is not reset again while it keeps answering. The version it
  publishes is the declared triple only: no project name, no `compileTime`, no
  `elfSha256`, so its update badge is decided the same way a Wi-Fi OTA's already is and
  a rebuild under an unchanged `version.txt` reads `same-version`. The seam `s.lanDial`
  is wired in `main.go` and deliberately nil in `newHub()` so the suite dials no
  network — which is why `TestTheRealHubWiresTheQuestionItIsNowOwedToAsk` pins the
  wiring by reading the source: a forgotten line there is invisible to every other test.
  Its built-in firmware is embedded at `make manager` time: after any
  `idf.py build`, refresh with `make firmware && make manager` or the hub's
  Update button silently downgrades the board. Started headless on this box
  as `bin/orb-manager -addr 127.0.0.1:3779 -no-window -probe-cables=false`.
- **Console / flashing moved to the native USB-JTAG port `/dev/ttyACM1`**
  (mac 44:BD:8D:78:26:18); the CH340 `ttyUSB0` path is gone. The CDC console
  streams **only to a freshly-opened, DTR-asserted handle**: `cat` shows
  nothing, and an esptool open/close detaches the stream. Working recipe:
  reset with `esptool --after no_reset chip_id`, then `esptool run` (or a
  hard reset) and open `/tmp/orb_serC.py` within ~200 ms — it timestamps
  lines and catches the 1 s/7 s/9 s boot ladder (appinstall, httpd, sta ip).
  esptool from `/home/greg/.espressif/python_env/idf5.4_py3.14_env/bin/`,
  storage partition read/write at `0xD60000`, 24 KB.
- **`esptool run` leaves the chip in DOWNLOAD mode** (`rst:0x15
  USB_UART_CHIP_RESET, boot:0x23 DOWNLOAD`): the dial goes black, the console
  goes silent, and it reads exactly like a boot-loop or a dead flash. Escape
  with `esptool --after hard_reset <cmd>`; confirm liveness first with
  `ping 192.168.2.34` before diagnosing firmware. The build environment is
  `~/esp/esp-idf` **v5.4** via `/tmp/buildB.sh` (build, serflash, 25 s serial
  capture, DONE marker) — the `/home/rocklinux/esp-idf-v5.5.2` path in older
  notes is wrong; the running image reports `idf: v5.4`.
- **The clock-app disappearance was self-inflicted by the 22:11 rebuilt
  `storage` image** (`/tmp/stor_new.bin`): it was regenerated containing
  only `orb.net`/`orb.idle`/`orb.grants`, so it silently dropped the
  `orb.app<slot>` enable words, `orb.os`, `tz`, and the whole `orb.fault`
  namespace — `appinstall: resolved 1`, dial lost clock. **Re-flashing the
  untouched pre-repair capture `/tmp/storB.bin` restored everything**
  (`enabled "clock" from its stored word`, 2 walkable apps, tz US/Central,
  idle 60 s) and did NOT restart the fault loop — its `orb.fault` counts
  were already zeroed and the torn slot had self-healed. Bench rule:
  **image surgery on `storage`, never image regeneration**; `nvs_tool.py`
  (IDF `nvs_flash/nvs_partition_tool/`, run under the idf5.4 venv python)
  dumps a capture to JSON so the edit is by-namespace and auditable.
- **The 4096→8192 `orb_jobs` stack is not why httpd dies.** A boot with the
  old 4096 worker still logged `ESP_ERR_HTTPD_TASK`; the reclaim build
  (worker 8192 + ota_buf/s_scan_json in PSRAM, `post-wifi_start
  largest=10240`) fails identically. The failing ask is the 8192 httpd
  task stack with ~3 KB of struct/ctx/semaphore/socket allocs taken before
  it — needs largest ≳ 12 KB at the boot instant. HEAD's .bss is 130,624 B
  vs 121,584 B at the last config-page-good boot: find those +9 KB first
  (theme-parser-era growth), then re-run the httpd-start ladder at
  12288/14336. The 8192 worker stays — the store-path overflow at 4096 is
  reproduced twice with a PC trace and the 8 KB figure is the documented
  plan fix.


## The USB Drive share: honest labels, and the window's own card — measured 2026-10-25

- **The press shares the card NOW. It never restarted to share** — the old row
  label "Restart to share disk" was a lie the code never executed. The reclaim
  restart is real (console and FatFs both return only at boot). The USB Drive
  row still labels its press the door during a window, because the hub's menu
  walk reads the row table, not the glass; on the dial the door no longer lives
  there (next bullet).
- **A share window freezes the glass on purpose**: `orb_usb_msc_start()` holds
  `display_dma_borrow()` for the whole window (the MSC storage object needs the
  ring's 8 KB as one DMA block; it cannot be re-funded while the mode lives), so
  every flush after the borrow is DROPPED (display.c `s_ring_parked` branch —
  dropped frames still call flush_ready, so the LVGL task and the 10 ms router
  poll keep running). The CO5300 holds its own GRAM: frozen, not blank.
- **The freeze now preserves the share card, not a dead menu** (owner ruling
  2026-10-25: the frozen row list read as "my orb is locked up"). `menu_view`
  owns `PG_SHARE`, a row-less card: "USB SHARED" at 48 px, one line saying the
  computer holds the card, and the door in words. `input_router`'s poll calls
  `menu_view_share_gate(pressed||lpress)` right after the idle-dim touch line and
  before any dispatch: while `orb_usb_msc_window_open()` (PENDING or ACTIVE) the
  card is painted once (homed to the menu first if some other screen was up, so
  even a boot-claim share's frozen frame carries words), every press or hold
  ANYWHERE posts the stop, and turns are swallowed — a cursor moving on a panel
  that cannot repaint is the knob looking broken. The page survives
  disarm/re-entry: `repaint()` paints the card from any armed state while
  `s_pg` is the share page, and `menu_view_set_armed` bows to the window.
- **New MSC_PENDING state is the press's word**: `orb_usb_msc_request_start()`
  flips it synchronously on the LVGL task BEFORE act_app's repaint, so the card
  (and the row's true exit text) land on the LAST frame the glass will get.
  job_start sleeps 150 ms before touching anything so that frame commits while
  the ring is still funded — two router polls of slack, by design. Failure
  paths reset PENDING→OFF: frames resume, and `menu_view_service` sees a share
  page under a closed window and repaints the menu live.
- **Stop says goodbye live, best effort**: `orb_usb_msc_stop` now sets
  `s_stopping` (state word `"stopping"`, card line "The orb is restarting"),
  `tud_disconnect()`s the host, deletes the storage LUN, uninstalls the MSC
  class and TinyUSB, and `display_dma_return()`s the ring — all inside the
  750 ms the reboot timer allows. With the ring refunded, the gate's per-poll
  repaint lands the closing card on a live panel before the boot; if the
  refund cannot be funded, the frozen card (already the honest one) simply
  holds until the reboot. No behavior regressed on failure; the volume stays
  the host's either way, and the card is never handed back mid-window.
- **Bench proof (usbip lead, WSL2, board Bronze on 192.168.2.32, 2026-10-25,
  card build)**: walked the menu by injector (cursor log lines confirmed rows),
  press started the share (log: handover → "tile ring borrowed 18640 B" →
  TinyUSB install → "exporting the whole card" → "host has configured the
  device"), and `/api/v1/fb?direct=1` during the window served the frozen
  mirror — pixel analysis says SHARE CARD, not frozen menu: a 1.9 k-px white
  band at the 48 px title, an amber wrapped status line, a grey door line at
  the hint, and ZERO blue-rectangle row-highlight pixels where the list used
  to be. ONE injected press then closed the ring exactly as designed:
  `rebooting out of disk mode` → `host let go of the card` (the detach is
  heard) → `tile ring back: 1 x 18640 B` one millisecond later (the farewell
  repaint window is real) → `rebooting in 750 ms` → clean boot, `usb off`,
  `sd mounted`. The old label-pass proof below stays as history.
- **Bench proof (usbip lead, WSL2, board on 192.168.2.32)**: walked the menu by
  injector (cursor log lines confirmed rows), press armed the share (log:
  handover → "tile ring borrowed 18640 B" → TinyUSB install → "exporting the
  whole card"), status flipped `host connected`, then ONE injected press rebooted
  the board back to normal (`usb.state: "off"`, clean boot, hub logged refusal,
  zero resets). `/api/v1/fb` cannot capture during a window ("capture buffer
  held by a commit" — the capture rides on commits, and none land); verify the
  frozen label's pixels with eyes on the bench.
- **usbip caveat**: the console lead is attached from the Windows host via
  usbipd-win. The MSC switch reconfigures the composite and the reclaim reboot
  detaches it — after a bench share test, re-attach from Windows
  (`usbipd attach --wsl -b <busid>`) before expecting /dev/ttyACM0. The hub
  handles the gone-port window correctly: probe fails with "no answer on file",
## The two invisible themes on orb-b — FORMAT 2 download names, measured 2026-10-25

- Symptom: orb-b (Gold, .34) card had two .orbtheme copied on, the device page said
  none. Boot log said `0 of 2 entries listed` and nothing else. The silence was the
  bug's other half: `orb_files_walk` counts every `.orbtheme` then **skips, unopened,
  any stem without the source's prefix** (`clock-` for the clock's themes source,
  clock_view.c), and a skip printed nothing. Now it prints
  `files: /sd/themes: hides "NAME" — the name lacks the clock-prefix` (orb_files.c).
  Read that line before believing an empty page.
- The two files were `ea5f6bdc79b8.orbtheme` + `32eddb138f42.orbtheme` =
  **sha256(name)[:12] of the bare title** — the FORMAT 2 id rule. Content was healthy
  FORMAT 3 ("Skeleton", "Open tourbillon"). Producer: the composer download AT
  `2d27e35^` — it baked FORMAT 3 bytes but named the download with the retired
  name-only hash (`stem = id || title`). `2d27e35` fixed the naming; a Chrome tab or
  cache predating that commit still shipped the old name. The hub's UPLOAD door never
  produces this: the firmware files every upload itself from the container header
  (upload_end → theme_stem), which is why a 409-duplicate path always lands compound.
- Repair loop, all of it over the bench doors: MSC share (menu walk + injected press,
  see the 10-25 USB section) → Windows auto-mounted the card as E: → renamed the two
  files to `orbtheme.card_name()`'s output (`clock-skeleton-209c630ff17d`,
  `clock-open-tourbillon-4cfdd4c0d959`) → ONE injected press on the frozen page ran
  the new opt_sel door: reboot, card re-mounted, usb.state off, and
  `2 of 2 entries listed` with both rows verified. A press during the window is the
  door on real hardware, not just in the test.
- Hub-side close of the remaining hole (browser without crypto.subtle — every
  plain-http non-localhost origin): `GET /api/theme/stem?name=` answers the compound
  stem from Go's own sha256 (`orbnet.ThemeIDOf`), gated by requireControl like the
  other theme routes; the composer's `cardStemFor` falls back to it before the raw
  title, so a hub-served download is now named the way the card files it, from any
  origin. `TestThemeStemNamesLikeTheDeviceFiles` pins the answer against orbnet's
  mirror of the firmware rule. The title fallback survives only for file:// opens.
- usbip truth from tonight: at MSC handover the composite reconfigures and the WSL
  attach DIES ("connection closed", busid vanishes from the export list while Windows
  claims the disk). After the reclaim reboot re-attach with
  `sudo usbip attach -r 127.0.0.1 -b <busid>` (the serial port and the MAC that names
  the board are in `usbip list --remote`). esptool while the hub also holds the port
  can blip once — a retry while the hub is between probes flashed clean.

  it does NOT reset (cableproof decline path).


## Headless boot, third witness — measured 2026-10-26, orb-Gold (v1.1.9)

- A theme apply rebooted Gold into a live dial and a closed door. Triage took
  five minutes it did not need: **`ip neigh` said REACHABLE at the right MAC
  while every TCP port timed out — ping-alive + connect-timeout + fresh mDNS
  announce = headless httpd, wifi fine.** Check ARP before believing "wifi
  seems down"; the hub card's offline verdict is a status-poll verdict, not a
  radio verdict.
- The failure was the 2026-10-24 squeeze recurring, and the day's captures
  complete its record. At t=2077 ms the ladder died `ESP_ERR_HTTPD_TASK` one
  millisecond after a self-initiated `auth-leave`, with `largest=7424` — the
  shield's freed 8192 block had already gone to the wifi re-init task on the
  other core. At t=32082 the +30 s retry died in `listen(112)` with 26 KB
  free: the leak from the single failed attempt, exactly as the 10-24 note
  predicts, and at ten table entries one leak plus mDNS's UDP sockets is the
  whole margin. The retry design only works on a bigger table.
- Fixed in v1.1.11 (e51dd86): `net_start_httpd()` moved above `esp_wifi_init`
  — bind() needs no association, pre-radio there is no churn to race and no
  handler of a later stage depends on the old ordering; and
  `CONFIG_LWIP_MAX_SOCKETS` 10 → 16 so the fd-leak budget survives one failed
  attempt and the timer's recovery can land. The heap shield stays as insurance
  against the fragmentation flavor (a board has been seen at 3,456 B largest
  with the door shut), the ladder fails fast on task-refusal, and
  `orb_net_start` no longer aborts on httpd failure — the 10-26 boot showed
  that abort also skipped the serial reader, which is the file's own
  never-fatal rule turned against a rescue.
- Confirming capture protocol after any boot-ordering change: serial is the
  only witness that cannot lie — DTR-open reset, 60 s minimum so a retry
  (t≈+30 s) is on tape, grep `shield released`, `httpd_start`, `head back`.
  A `largest=` figure on a ladder line measures the instant after the failed
  attempt's own cleanup, not the attempt; trust the DMA-largest print.

## The fb door's held arm, and the switch probe that now heals a stalled glass — measured 2026-10-27, orb-B

- **The `/api/v1/fb` door used to wedge itself, and the wedge was one line's
  absence.** A capture is ARMED and filled by the next LVGL commit; a poll that
  timed out returned 504 and left the arm held, forever. On a screen that
  stopped repainting that made every later capture inherit the wedge —
  `display_capture_direct()` refuses against a held arm by design, so the door
  denied its own documented fallback for two hours. Fixed by
  `display_capture_disarm()` (ARMED→IDLE only; a landed READY frame belongs to
  whoever armed it) called at every post-arm exit of `h_fb`. A request against
  a static screen is the live regression: 504, then `?direct=1` must serve
  torn, where the old build answered 503.
- **A switch probe miss is a verdict only after it has been asked for an
  excuse.** While the tile ring is lent to the card the flush path drops
  frames ON PURPOSE, so bench counters frozen mid-SD-write is a healthy board,
  not a stall — and a heal that rebooted there would reboot every install.
  `display_dma_lent()` is the excuse query; `SW_LEND_MAX_MS 10000` bounds it.
- **The stall heal reboots, and the fuse is "any frame ever."** The probe runs
  on the LVGL task, so a firing probe proves the task alive and the flush path
  the dead thing — there is no in-place re-init to reach; `esp_restart()` after
  `orb_svc_note_clean_reboot()` is the recovery the holder can get without a
  cable, and the NVS resume page puts them back on the face that froze. A board
  that has committed ZERO frames since boot has a dead panel instead, and
  rebooting into it forever is a loop — that case logs and waits for a human.


## The upload door erased the face it was protecting — D9's arm moved into the store, measured 2026-10-27, orb-B (v1.1.24)

- **One aborted POST cost the theme.** D9 armed the slot by erasing its header
  at `orb_theme_upload_begin()` — before a payload byte crossed — so every
  stalled, killed or refused upload left the slot headerless: `art_ready`
  false, base face, no fallback row on the card. A killed 1.2 MB curl proved it
  twice that day, and it is the whole of the owner's "send fails at 95% and I
  end up with a dead face".
- **The invariant is what moved, not what died.** A torn store must still boot
  the base face, never a half-mapped image: the header erase now lives in
  `copy_bundle3()`, FIRST, ahead of the payload erase it already performed.
  That point is past every check, past the park, and local flash — no radio on
  the critical path. A refused upload now leaves the live theme byte-intact.
  `host_sd_test.py`'s erase-ordering lints pass unchanged; they read
  `orb_theme_install_bundle()`'s body, and the ordered erases sit inside the
  copy it pins before.
- **Bench proof, in order**: store round-trip (POST /sd/bundle -> reboot ->
  source slot, Modern active) with v1.1.24; then the same timeout-killed curl
  that flipped art_ready false on v1.1.23 leaves it TRUE, and the capture shows
  the dial resumed (`switch clock -> glass +420 ms`) instead of a base face.
  The same capture shows the v1.1.23 lend-excuse line during the card hold —
  the stall-heal took the card's explanation and did NOT reboot, which is
  exactly the behavior that excuse exists for.
- **Hub-side fragility the same session proved, for context on "first attempt
  errors out"**: the device admits 3 sockets and lru-purges; the hub's
  dead-conn redial in devicePostCounted exists for exactly the RST that eats a
  first attempt when a status poll or a rebooted board kills the socket
  mid-write. That layer was healthy; what made it look broken was the arm,
  because a retry that ALSO hiccuped left no face to show for it.

## The Themes page killed its own lane — the preview stream must die with its render, 2026-10-27 (hub, Windows)

- **"orb busy" on a page open is the page fighting its own zombie.** The hub's
  lane releases only when the relay's socket closes. `clearPreviewQueue` bumped
  the generation and dropped the QUEUE but left the in-flight preview fetch
  streaming, so the redrawn page's own `/api/theme/list` — a control dial with
  a 1.5 s budget deliberately under one cold preview's ~4 s — collided with the
  dead render's thumbnail and painted the 503 as if the orb had died.
- **The fix is two halves in app.js**: the in-flight preview carries an
  AbortController and `clearPreviewQueue` fires it, so re-render frees the hub
  lane at once; and `themeListWithRetry` rides out a genuine lane-busy (3 x
  2.3 s, the error string is the test) before surfacing it. The ops_test abort
  pin moved 1 -> 2 with this reason written into it; a flash fetch is STILL
  banned from aborting.
- **The board reboots during that session's browsing were the bench, not the
  build**: capf.py RESETS the orb when it opens the port (its own docstring
  says so; status shows rst=11 "usb" for it). Every per-trial capture kicked
  the board mid-page. Capture ONCE per bench session and segment the log; open
  the port while the person is browsing is never acceptable.

## The stall heal burned a healthy store — the card's own lock replaced the guessed bound, 2026-10-27, orb-B (v1.1.25)

- **v1.1.24's heal was the sender's executioner.** The owner's report: send
  lands, "copying" shows, board resets, theme never arrives, the hub shows a
  connection-reset on the upload POST. The lend-excuse carried a fixed
  SW_LEND_MAX_MS=10000 — a guessed ceiling the comment admitted nothing had
  measured. A real store of a 1.2 MB bake on a real radio crosses it while the
  card works perfectly, the excuse expired, and the probe esp_restart-ed
  through a live copy. My bench "proof" streamed a small bake at LAN speed and
  never crossed 10 s; the owner's network made every send cross it.
- **The excuse is now the card's own activity, not a wall clock.**
  orb_sd_lock_idle_ms() (new) answers "how long since ANY task held the card
  lock"; a chunked writer refreshes it every few hundred ms and a whole-store
  hold reads 0 forever. input_router re-arms while idle < 5 s or the ring is
  lent, with NO total-silence ceiling — the sdmmc command timeouts bound the
  card, not a #define. And a hard second fuse: the heal never fires while the
  lock is held. A wedged holder deserves a log and a cable; rebooting through
  the mutex is how the FAT gets torn.
- **Bench sequence, v1.1.25**: full store round-trip (copying -> storing ->
  stored, rebooting, rst=0xc RTC_SW, slot maps 1175552 B); then the regression:
  --limit-rate 40k upload, killed at T+13 s of silence, 30% past the old fuse —
  serial shows "silent with the card at work (lock held 1555 ms)", the dial
  resumes after the abort, art_ready TRUE, bytes unchanged. Killed-upload with
  no face loss now holds past the fuse window that v1.1.24 died in.
- **First-press "could not get a clean read" is the page eating its lane.**
  The Theme page's preview relays hold the hub's lane for seconds; the precheck
  ThemeList is a control dial with a 1.5 s budget — the first press collides
  with the page's own in-flight thumbnail and the old one-retry shape called
  that a dirty read. themeListRidingOutLane waits the lane out (6 x 1.5 s on
  ErrLaneBusy only); app.js's list read got the same quiet-retry, and a
  re-render now ABORTS the in-flight preview (ops_test pin moved 1->2).

## The clock editor timed itself out at the last inch of its own budget — 2026-10-27, orb-B

- **Measured, then fixed**: GET /api/v1/apps/editor?id=clock answers 640 KB in
  6.03 s cold and 8.70 s warm on the bench LAN. The hub dialled it on the
  management client — deviceTimeout 8 s — so an honest read died at the last
  inch and the browser showed the Go text "context deadline exceeded ... while
  reading body". Same mistake class previewTimeout (45 s) and otaTimeout (180 s)
  each document: a single-task httpd streaming a big verified region is not a
  management call. fetchEditor now rides s.preview: the 45 s budget, bulk lane
  flag, and firstByte 8 s that still kills a socket-open-then-silent board.
- **The recovery gaps were worse than the timeout.** A failed region memoised
  for its full 15 s TTL, so Retry re-served the same dead error; and the 502
  arrived as raw JSON inside the iframe — no name, no button, close-the-pane as
  the only exit. Now: transport failures memoise 2 s (ErrNoEditor stays 15 s —
  a stable answer costs nothing to re-read); editorRegion retries a fresh
  transport failure once at 400 ms; and editorRefused answers a document
  navigation (Accept: text/html) with an HTML page that names the failure and
  carries a Retry anchor, no-store, no token. Member fetches keep the JSON.
- Pinned by four tests in editor_test.go: retry-then-serve counts dials exactly
  (2), ErrNoEditor never retried (1), the HTML page carries Retry+reason and no
  token, and the aged short memo re-dials a recovered board.

## Two hubs on one box refused the operator's own press — the flock has no process boundary, 2026-10-29 (hub, :3779 + :3791)

**Symptom on the bench.** A first-flash write and a Wi-Fi send both refused on
`/dev/ttyACM2` with *"port is busy - another flash or provision holds its lock"*.
Nothing was on the wire, and the board was blank, so the retry answered
`not an orb` while the refusal kept insisting a writer existed.

**Cause.** Two `orb-manager` processes were live: the rebuilt hub on `:3779` and a
dev hub on `:3791`. Each runs its own background sweep, and a sweep round holds the
port's `flock` for the whole round. `3e33f0a` taught a press to evict a sweep
yesterday, but the operation registry is per-process. A press in one hub cannot
cancel a sweep in the other, so it took the plain refusal. A blank chip never earns
the `IsOrb` identity that makes `proveCable` stand down (`cableproof.go:382`), so
both hubs kept asking forever. Decisive line: the refusal appears five times in the
`:3791` log (first at 11:08:20, 32 s after it launched, before any rebuild) and zero
times in the `:3779` log. The hub losing the race was always the one the operator
pressed.

**What shipped.** `internal/orbbind/press.go` adds two advisory flags per canonical
port, ordered press → sweep → flock. `<port>.press` is held for a whole write, and
every sweep checks it first, so a human ends background work on that cable in every
process. `<port>.sweep` is the lease: one hub sweeps a cable at a time, the loser
skips the round. `PressPort` (flash, lonely, provision, cmd, console, `serprov`)
waits up to `pressWindow` 12 s for the one round already in flight. `SweepPort`
(cable proof, probes, `orbprobe`) declines instead of racing. `LockPort` is unchanged,
so the theme store pass and older hubs keep shipped semantics. Press-vs-press still
refuses at once: two writers on one wire corrupts the write.

**Still true, so check it first.** One hub per box. Run `pgrep -af orb-manager` before
a bench pass. A round already past its flag check holds the flock until its own budget
ends — 8 s for a proof, up to 40 s for a probe — so a press can still wait the window
and read the busy line. The guard makes two hubs lose politely; it does not make them
correct.

## A sweep or a tick on a STILL dial shipped 1 Hz — 2026-10-30 (composer, baker, fw)

- **Symptom on Bronze.** The Station's seconds hand stepped once a second while the
  composer's Motion row read "Sweep" and the dial animated smoothly in the pane. The
  log said `clock 4 fps` and `sweep on`. Same for any dial that bakes to a still
  plate: Station, Aviator, White, Silver, Sector.
- **Cause.** `bakeClock3` defaults the cadence to `Math.max(bank fps, tourbillon fps,
  1)`. A still plate carries no bank and no carriage, so it shipped `tick_rate` 1
  against `second_sweep` 1. The scheduler treats a sweep as a FLUID ask and clamps it
  into `[4..15]`, so the dial ran at the panel floor: 250 ms per sample of a wall-clock
  fraction. `clock_view.c`'s boot line printed the bare word `on`, so the log hid the
  figure that mattered.
- **Why the Aviator swept and the Station did not.** Not the preset. The Aviator on the
  glass was the FACTORY bundle (`assets/orb_default.img`, header name `Aviator`), baked
  by `tools/bake_clock.py --tick-rate 15 --force-sweep` per `assets/sources/av-kit/
  README.md`: its scalars read `second_sweep 1, tick_rate 15, bg_mode 1, gif_frames 15`,
  and GIF mode drives the whole dial off the bank's own 67 ms delays. A composer FORMAT 3
  bake of the SAME Aviator preset ships `tick_rate` 1 and ticks too. Any dial whose bake
  names a tempo (the factory kit, or anything with a plate bank or a carriage) sweeps;
  a still composer bake named none, and the 1 Hz floor answered for it.
- **The fix, three seats.** `orbtheme-baker.js` (and the hub's copy) floors a declared
  sweep with no tempo on the wire at `SWEEP_TIER3` 15; bank and carriage tempos keep
  Oct 23's "fastest on the wire" rule verbatim, and an author word still wins.
  `Watch Composer.dc.html` names the beat for a still carrier from `devFps()` — the
  number the preview ticked at — and prints it on the Motion row as `ships 15 bps
  sweep`, so the pane cannot promise a look the wire does not carry. Naming a scalar is
  what makes the send honest: `serialContent3` folds `opts.scalars`, so a cadence-only
  edit moves the serial and the hub's precheck re-sends instead of calling a different
  file "the exact theme" (that suite's §37).
- **A tick on a still dial was inert too.** `orb_sched_tick_base_ms()` returned 1000 ms
  for any non-sweep theme, so a 4/s choice on a still dial drew one of four quantised
  steps once a second. It now honours a named `tick_rate` above 1 on every art class; a
  theme naming nothing keeps the classic 1 Hz. `gfx_parity_test.py` carries the new
  `ACCEPTED` entry with the reason — the extraction promise held, this routine changed
  on purpose after it.
- **The boot line now says the number.** `sweep on(15 bps, base 66 ms)` on a plate or
  still dial, `off(4 bps, base 250 ms)` on a ticking one, and the GIF branches keep their
  `every N ticks` form. `base` is the beat the ladder starts from; the running
  `clock %u fps` line stays the answer to what the glass held.
- **Measured.** `tools/watch-composer/_selftest/headless-bundle3.test.js`: 201 checks,
  four of them the new cadence vectors (still + sweep → 15, author 8 → 8, author 4
  ticking → 4, animated bank → unchanged). `tools/host_sched_rung_test.py`: 21 vectors,
  mutants 7/7. Re-baked Station through real Chrome (`bank3-drive.html?dial=station`):
  `second_sweep=1 tick_rate=15`, 1 446 758 B, serial moved. Python's mirrored
  `pick_rung` answers 15 for `(15 fluid, floor 15)` where it answered 4 for the old
  `(1 fluid, floor 15)`, and still answers 12 for a 4 fps bank under a 15 ask, so the
  bank never aliases. A sweep across live plates costs power per §40's table; this
  changes the Station's cadence, no bank, no new power claim measured on glass.
